WEBVTT

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:01.928 align:middle line:90%
[SQUEAKING]

00:00:01.928 --> 00:00:04.338 align:middle line:90%
[RUSTLING]

00:00:04.338 --> 00:00:07.712 align:middle line:90%
[CLICKING]

00:00:07.712 --> 00:00:10.998 align:middle line:90%


00:00:10.998 --> 00:00:13.290 align:middle line:84%
NORVIN RICHARDS: So last
time, we started doing syntax,

00:00:13.290 --> 00:00:18.240 align:middle line:84%
and we had started drawing
trees like this one.

00:00:18.240 --> 00:00:21.240 align:middle line:90%
What I said, I think, was--

00:00:21.240 --> 00:00:22.823 align:middle line:84%
if you remember when
we were looking--

00:00:22.823 --> 00:00:25.240 align:middle line:84%
when we were doing morphology
and we were looking at words

00:00:25.240 --> 00:00:27.570 align:middle line:84%
like "unlockable," we were
convincing ourselves that it

00:00:27.570 --> 00:00:32.490 align:middle line:84%
was profitable to think of
words like that as not just

00:00:32.490 --> 00:00:35.250 align:middle line:90%
consisting of three morphemes--

00:00:35.250 --> 00:00:37.722 align:middle line:90%
"UN-LOCK-ABLE."

00:00:37.722 --> 00:00:39.180 align:middle line:84%
It does consist of
three morphemes,

00:00:39.180 --> 00:00:41.640 align:middle line:84%
but that it's useful to think
of these three morphemes

00:00:41.640 --> 00:00:45.310 align:middle line:90%
as being composed in pairs.

00:00:45.310 --> 00:00:48.690 align:middle line:84%
So there are two ways to
make the word "unlockable."

00:00:48.690 --> 00:00:50.400 align:middle line:84%
You can put "un-"
together with "lock,"

00:00:50.400 --> 00:00:53.025 align:middle line:84%
and then you can plop the result
of that together with "-able,"

00:00:53.025 --> 00:00:55.023 align:middle line:84%
or you can put "lock"
together with "-able,"

00:00:55.023 --> 00:00:56.940 align:middle line:84%
and put the result of
that together with "un-"

00:00:56.940 --> 00:00:59.760 align:middle line:90%
and those mean different things.

00:00:59.760 --> 00:01:01.530 align:middle line:84%
That was what we were
saying when we were

00:01:01.530 --> 00:01:03.330 align:middle line:90%
talking about "unlockable."

00:01:03.330 --> 00:01:07.770 align:middle line:84%
We're going to talk about
syntax the same way.

00:01:07.770 --> 00:01:09.960 align:middle line:90%
Yes, this is a string of words--

00:01:09.960 --> 00:01:12.270 align:middle line:90%
however many words it is--

00:01:12.270 --> 00:01:15.930 align:middle line:84%
8, I guess-- but
there's more to say.

00:01:15.930 --> 00:01:17.680 align:middle line:84%
It isn't just a
string of eight words.

00:01:17.680 --> 00:01:19.770 align:middle line:84%
It's a string of 8
words that are composed

00:01:19.770 --> 00:01:22.437 align:middle line:84%
via this pairwise
operation of merge that

00:01:22.437 --> 00:01:24.270 align:middle line:84%
takes pairs of things
and puts them together

00:01:24.270 --> 00:01:27.008 align:middle line:90%
to form larger things.

00:01:27.008 --> 00:01:28.050 align:middle line:90%
The other thing we said--

00:01:28.050 --> 00:01:32.760 align:middle line:84%
so I've just gotten started on
building a syntactic structure

00:01:32.760 --> 00:01:34.510 align:middle line:84%
for this sentence
here by saying,

00:01:34.510 --> 00:01:37.050 align:middle line:84%
yeah, we've got these
two nouns, "book"

00:01:37.050 --> 00:01:40.750 align:middle line:84%
and "garage," which are
preceded by determiner

00:01:40.750 --> 00:01:43.500 align:middle line:84%
"the," which should be merged
together with these nouns

00:01:43.500 --> 00:01:46.200 align:middle line:90%
to form larger objects.

00:01:46.200 --> 00:01:52.650 align:middle line:84%
In the case of "unlockable," we
were saying affixes like "un-"

00:01:52.650 --> 00:01:56.280 align:middle line:84%
are going to come with
instructions like "Put me

00:01:56.280 --> 00:01:59.530 align:middle line:84%
together with a verb and
I'll give you a verb."

00:01:59.530 --> 00:02:03.218 align:middle line:84%
And instructions-- sorry,
morphemes like "-able"

00:02:03.218 --> 00:02:05.760 align:middle line:84%
are going to come together with
instructions that say "Put me

00:02:05.760 --> 00:02:08.740 align:middle line:84%
together with a verb and I
will give you an adjective."

00:02:08.740 --> 00:02:14.370 align:middle line:84%
And so when we put two things
together, we're adding these--

00:02:14.370 --> 00:02:19.140 align:middle line:84%
putting labels on these nodes
that we create via merge.

00:02:19.140 --> 00:02:21.570 align:middle line:84%
In the case of
morphology, what label

00:02:21.570 --> 00:02:26.460 align:middle line:84%
you put on the larger node
is determined by the affixes.

00:02:26.460 --> 00:02:29.190 align:middle line:84%
And we decided affixes have
to come with instructions,

00:02:29.190 --> 00:02:31.650 align:middle line:84%
like "I turn verbs
into adjectives,"

00:02:31.650 --> 00:02:34.800 align:middle line:84%
or "I attach to adjectives, and
the result is an adjective"--

00:02:34.800 --> 00:02:36.330 align:middle line:90%
things like that.

00:02:36.330 --> 00:02:39.630 align:middle line:84%
For syntax,
similarly, we're going

00:02:39.630 --> 00:02:42.105 align:middle line:84%
to take these pairs
of things and we're

00:02:42.105 --> 00:02:43.980 align:middle line:84%
going to merge them
together, and we're going

00:02:43.980 --> 00:02:46.710 align:middle line:90%
to give a label to the result.

00:02:46.710 --> 00:02:48.570 align:middle line:84%
In syntax, the
label of the result

00:02:48.570 --> 00:02:51.030 align:middle line:84%
is always going to be the
label one of the two things

00:02:51.030 --> 00:02:52.540 align:middle line:90%
that you put together.

00:02:52.540 --> 00:02:55.530 align:middle line:84%
So in this case here
where I merged "the"

00:02:55.530 --> 00:02:59.100 align:middle line:84%
and "book" or I merged
"the" and "garage," I'm

00:02:59.100 --> 00:03:01.500 align:middle line:84%
going to give the
result of that the label

00:03:01.500 --> 00:03:05.718 align:middle line:84%
"N," because we decided
that the rules--

00:03:05.718 --> 00:03:08.010 align:middle line:84%
whatever they are-- for
determining which things can go

00:03:08.010 --> 00:03:11.280 align:middle line:90%
where seem to care about the N.

00:03:11.280 --> 00:03:14.010 align:middle line:84%
That is, there are a
variety of things--

00:03:14.010 --> 00:03:17.040 align:middle line:90%
take a sentence like this one--

00:03:17.040 --> 00:03:20.230 align:middle line:84%
that can go in the
slot occupied here by,

00:03:20.230 --> 00:03:22.118 align:middle line:90%
for example, "the book."

00:03:22.118 --> 00:03:24.660 align:middle line:84%
And what they have in common is
that they have nouns in them.

00:03:24.660 --> 00:03:26.910 align:middle line:84%
So you can say "I will find
the book in the garage,"

00:03:26.910 --> 00:03:29.820 align:middle line:84%
you can say "I will find books
in the garage," you can say

00:03:29.820 --> 00:03:32.070 align:middle line:84%
"I will find purple
books in the garage,"

00:03:32.070 --> 00:03:34.505 align:middle line:84%
you can say "I will find the
purple books about syntax

00:03:34.505 --> 00:03:35.130 align:middle line:90%
in the garage."

00:03:35.130 --> 00:03:36.300 align:middle line:84%
There are various
kinds of modifiers

00:03:36.300 --> 00:03:37.675 align:middle line:84%
and other things
that you can add

00:03:37.675 --> 00:03:39.360 align:middle line:84%
or not, but what they
all have in common

00:03:39.360 --> 00:03:41.050 align:middle line:90%
is that there's a noun.

00:03:41.050 --> 00:03:44.160 align:middle line:84%
And so we're going
to reflect that fact

00:03:44.160 --> 00:03:46.650 align:middle line:90%
by naming that larger object--

00:03:46.650 --> 00:03:49.200 align:middle line:84%
the result of merging
"the" with book--

00:03:49.200 --> 00:03:53.130 align:middle line:84%
giving it the label "N" as
well, passing on the fact

00:03:53.130 --> 00:03:57.810 align:middle line:84%
that there's a noun
in here to that node.

00:03:57.810 --> 00:04:00.840 align:middle line:84%
In the case of
morphology, like I said,

00:04:00.840 --> 00:04:04.680 align:middle line:84%
we had nice clear
rules for what label

00:04:04.680 --> 00:04:07.060 align:middle line:90%
to put on each of these nodes.

00:04:07.060 --> 00:04:10.440 align:middle line:84%
The rule was look at the affix
and the affix will tell you

00:04:10.440 --> 00:04:11.440 align:middle line:90%
what you should do.

00:04:11.440 --> 00:04:14.940 align:middle line:84%
So the affixes say things like,
if you merge me with a verb,

00:04:14.940 --> 00:04:17.880 align:middle line:84%
the result will be a verb, or
if you merge me with a verb,

00:04:17.880 --> 00:04:20.700 align:middle line:90%
the result will be an adjective.

00:04:20.700 --> 00:04:22.860 align:middle line:84%
Syntacticians have
not gotten that far

00:04:22.860 --> 00:04:26.050 align:middle line:84%
with figuring out the rules
for how to label things,

00:04:26.050 --> 00:04:28.050 align:middle line:84%
so I am just going
to label things.

00:04:28.050 --> 00:04:31.620 align:middle line:84%
And this is a current topic of
fairly hot debate, actually,

00:04:31.620 --> 00:04:33.330 align:middle line:84%
among syntacticians--
like, why are we

00:04:33.330 --> 00:04:34.230 align:middle line:90%
getting the labels that we are?

00:04:34.230 --> 00:04:36.188 align:middle line:84%
We're pretty clear on
what the label should be,

00:04:36.188 --> 00:04:39.490 align:middle line:90%
but why is not as clear.

00:04:39.490 --> 00:04:43.080 align:middle line:90%
So that was one part of the--

00:04:43.080 --> 00:04:46.520 align:middle line:84%
start of building a
tree for this sentence.

00:04:46.520 --> 00:04:48.040 align:middle line:84%
Are there any
questions about this?

00:04:48.040 --> 00:04:51.960 align:middle line:90%
This is all quasi-review.

00:04:51.960 --> 00:04:56.160 align:middle line:84%
So from here, we've created
"the book" and "the garage."

00:04:56.160 --> 00:05:00.180 align:middle line:84%
We decided "in the garage"
is also a constituent.

00:05:00.180 --> 00:05:01.560 align:middle line:90%
It deserves a node.

00:05:01.560 --> 00:05:04.230 align:middle line:84%
It's a prepositional phrase,
so we're going to merge

00:05:04.230 --> 00:05:09.540 align:middle line:84%
"in" together with the node
that we created by-- that first

00:05:09.540 --> 00:05:11.810 align:middle line:84%
instance of merge,
"the garage"-- that N--

00:05:11.810 --> 00:05:14.990 align:middle line:84%
that larger N. Yeah, so we'll
merge P with that larger N

00:05:14.990 --> 00:05:19.850 align:middle line:84%
and we'll get a larger P--
a phrase with the label P--

00:05:19.850 --> 00:05:22.460 align:middle line:84%
what you could call a
prepositional phrase.

00:05:22.460 --> 00:05:25.700 align:middle line:84%
And then we said
similarly, "find"--

00:05:25.700 --> 00:05:30.270 align:middle line:84%
we're going to merge "find"
together first with "the book,"

00:05:30.270 --> 00:05:33.380 align:middle line:84%
and then finally, merge
those two things together

00:05:33.380 --> 00:05:36.080 align:middle line:84%
to get this structure for
that much of the sentence.

00:05:36.080 --> 00:05:37.665 align:middle line:90%
And we'll stop here for now.

00:05:37.665 --> 00:05:40.290 align:middle line:84%
We're going to build the rest of
the sentence in just a second.

00:05:40.290 --> 00:05:41.810 align:middle line:84%
And I think this was
as far as we got.

00:05:41.810 --> 00:05:44.030 align:middle line:84%
And I said, is anybody
alarmed or disturbed by this.

00:05:44.030 --> 00:05:47.370 align:middle line:84%
And several of you raised
your hands, because, in fact,

00:05:47.370 --> 00:05:50.300 align:middle line:84%
this is only one of a couple of
possible structures this tree

00:05:50.300 --> 00:05:51.500 align:middle line:90%
can have that's--

00:05:51.500 --> 00:05:53.960 align:middle line:84%
sorry, this is only one of a
couple of possible structures

00:05:53.960 --> 00:05:55.860 align:middle line:90%
this sentence can have.

00:05:55.860 --> 00:05:58.363 align:middle line:84%
We're going to spend a lot of
time talking about that today.

00:05:58.363 --> 00:06:00.780 align:middle line:84%
You were one of the people who
raised your hand last time.

00:06:00.780 --> 00:06:02.030 align:middle line:90%
Yeah?

00:06:02.030 --> 00:06:04.155 align:middle line:84%
AUDIENCE: So we
have P for both N

00:06:04.155 --> 00:06:06.380 align:middle line:84%
and raised in the
variety [INAUDIBLE]..

00:06:06.380 --> 00:06:10.490 align:middle line:84%
So is N by itself a
prepositional phrase

00:06:10.490 --> 00:06:12.570 align:middle line:90%
or is it just verb phrase?

00:06:12.570 --> 00:06:16.270 align:middle line:84%
NORVIN RICHARDS:
So good question.

00:06:16.270 --> 00:06:18.460 align:middle line:84%
Let's see if I can
answer your question

00:06:18.460 --> 00:06:23.020 align:middle line:84%
in a way that will not
cause more confusion than it

00:06:23.020 --> 00:06:25.990 align:middle line:90%
gets rid of.

00:06:25.990 --> 00:06:28.930 align:middle line:84%
I have used now the phrase
"prepositional phrase"

00:06:28.930 --> 00:06:34.777 align:middle line:84%
a couple of times, and "noun
phrase," and "verb phrase,"

00:06:34.777 --> 00:06:36.610 align:middle line:84%
and I'm going to
dramatically introduce what

00:06:36.610 --> 00:06:37.912 align:middle line:90%
those mean in just a second.

00:06:37.912 --> 00:06:39.370 align:middle line:84%
I think it's almost
the next slide.

00:06:39.370 --> 00:06:41.370 align:middle line:84%
Let me just ask you to
hang on to that question.

00:06:41.370 --> 00:06:43.540 align:middle line:90%
It's a good question.

00:06:43.540 --> 00:06:46.165 align:middle line:84%
But are there other questions
about this much-- this far?

00:06:46.165 --> 00:06:48.740 align:middle line:90%


00:06:48.740 --> 00:06:50.740 align:middle line:84%
So if you're looking at
this and thinking, wait,

00:06:50.740 --> 00:06:52.690 align:middle line:84%
I think of it as having a
different structure, that's

00:06:52.690 --> 00:06:53.648 align:middle line:90%
a good thought to have.

00:06:53.648 --> 00:06:55.880 align:middle line:90%
Hang on to that thought.

00:06:55.880 --> 00:06:57.523 align:middle line:84%
Ah, good-- this is
the answer to your--

00:06:57.523 --> 00:06:58.940 align:middle line:84%
I'm sorry, what's
your name again?

00:06:58.940 --> 00:06:59.750 align:middle line:90%
AUDIENCE: Kirai.

00:06:59.750 --> 00:07:00.500 align:middle line:90%
NORVIN RICHARDS: Say it again?

00:07:00.500 --> 00:07:01.125 align:middle line:90%
AUDIENCE: Kirai

00:07:01.125 --> 00:07:02.390 align:middle line:90%
NORVIN RICHARDS: Kirai.

00:07:02.390 --> 00:07:06.710 align:middle line:84%
Kirai's question-- so he
wanted to know, what do you

00:07:06.710 --> 00:07:08.390 align:middle line:90%
mean when you say phrase?

00:07:08.390 --> 00:07:12.410 align:middle line:84%
There's a convention,
and it's really just

00:07:12.410 --> 00:07:16.520 align:middle line:84%
to make it easier to
look at these trees.

00:07:16.520 --> 00:07:19.633 align:middle line:84%
But it's a very
widespread convention,

00:07:19.633 --> 00:07:21.800 align:middle line:84%
and I'll be doing it all
the time when I draw trees,

00:07:21.800 --> 00:07:26.240 align:middle line:90%
so let me introduce you to it.

00:07:26.240 --> 00:07:28.590 align:middle line:84%
If you have a tree-- so
the tree we had before--

00:07:28.590 --> 00:07:30.200 align:middle line:90%
let's go back a slide.

00:07:30.200 --> 00:07:32.360 align:middle line:90%
The tree we had before-- whoa--

00:07:32.360 --> 00:07:34.760 align:middle line:84%
had various nodes that
have the same label.

00:07:34.760 --> 00:07:37.460 align:middle line:84%
There are three nodes here that
have the label V, for example.

00:07:37.460 --> 00:07:40.340 align:middle line:84%
And there are two nodes
that have the label P,

00:07:40.340 --> 00:07:43.880 align:middle line:84%
and there are four that
have the label N, actually.

00:07:43.880 --> 00:07:45.470 align:middle line:84%
One-- two associated
with "garage"

00:07:45.470 --> 00:07:47.180 align:middle line:90%
and two associated with "book."

00:07:47.180 --> 00:07:50.660 align:middle line:84%
And if you look at this tree,
it can be fairly confusing

00:07:50.660 --> 00:07:53.840 align:middle line:84%
looking at all of these nodes
that have identical labels.

00:07:53.840 --> 00:07:56.660 align:middle line:84%
So the convention--
one convention

00:07:56.660 --> 00:07:59.090 align:middle line:84%
just for making it
easier to look at trees

00:07:59.090 --> 00:08:02.755 align:middle line:84%
is to mark the highest node that
has a given label when you have

00:08:02.755 --> 00:08:04.130 align:middle line:84%
a sequence of
nodes that all have

00:08:04.130 --> 00:08:07.220 align:middle line:84%
the same label, because they
all started with one word

00:08:07.220 --> 00:08:10.197 align:middle line:84%
and they're inheriting
a label from that word.

00:08:10.197 --> 00:08:12.030 align:middle line:84%
When you get to the
highest of those things,

00:08:12.030 --> 00:08:15.800 align:middle line:84%
you give it the label P,
where P stands for phrase.

00:08:15.800 --> 00:08:20.960 align:middle line:84%
So the answer to this question--
when we say a prepositional

00:08:20.960 --> 00:08:24.590 align:middle line:84%
phrase, what we typically
mean is the largest thing with

00:08:24.590 --> 00:08:26.690 align:middle line:90%
the label P from a given--

00:08:26.690 --> 00:08:27.650 align:middle line:90%
from a given node.

00:08:27.650 --> 00:08:30.230 align:middle line:84%
So "in" by itself
is a preposition,

00:08:30.230 --> 00:08:32.179 align:middle line:84%
and that larger thing
with the label P

00:08:32.179 --> 00:08:35.080 align:middle line:90%
is the prepositional phrase.

00:08:35.080 --> 00:08:37.690 align:middle line:84%
Again, this is just to make
it easier to process trees

00:08:37.690 --> 00:08:39.429 align:middle line:84%
so we won't see so
many identical labels.

00:08:39.429 --> 00:08:41.110 align:middle line:84%
We're just putting
a mark saying,

00:08:41.110 --> 00:08:43.073 align:middle line:84%
this is as far as
this label got.

00:08:43.073 --> 00:08:43.990 align:middle line:90%
That's all that means.

00:08:43.990 --> 00:08:45.212 align:middle line:90%
Yeah?

00:08:45.212 --> 00:08:48.060 align:middle line:90%
AUDIENCE: [INAUDIBLE]

00:08:48.060 --> 00:08:51.097 align:middle line:90%


00:08:51.097 --> 00:08:51.930 align:middle line:90%
NORVIN RICHARDS: Ah.

00:08:51.930 --> 00:08:54.810 align:middle line:84%
So in this tree, it's
not because there

00:08:54.810 --> 00:08:58.380 align:middle line:84%
is another thing with the
label V that is higher

00:08:58.380 --> 00:09:00.150 align:middle line:90%
than "find the book"-- yes.

00:09:00.150 --> 00:09:02.820 align:middle line:84%
But you are right--
there is another thing

00:09:02.820 --> 00:09:04.360 align:middle line:90%
we do for exactly that case.

00:09:04.360 --> 00:09:05.640 align:middle line:90%
Let me show you that.

00:09:05.640 --> 00:09:08.070 align:middle line:84%
Nice question-- it
allows me to segue nicely

00:09:08.070 --> 00:09:09.460 align:middle line:90%
into the next slide.

00:09:09.460 --> 00:09:12.900 align:middle line:84%
We have another thing that
we do for exactly that case.

00:09:12.900 --> 00:09:15.242 align:middle line:90%
Nodes that are not--

00:09:15.242 --> 00:09:17.700 align:middle line:84%
that don't get the label P,
because they're not the highest

00:09:17.700 --> 00:09:20.520 align:middle line:90%
thing and that also are not--

00:09:20.520 --> 00:09:22.620 align:middle line:90%
just the bear word, like find--

00:09:22.620 --> 00:09:24.900 align:middle line:84%
just the verb-- things
that are in between that.

00:09:24.900 --> 00:09:27.720 align:middle line:90%
It's an unfortunate label.

00:09:27.720 --> 00:09:34.200 align:middle line:84%
We call things like-- so
I'll draw it again here--

00:09:34.200 --> 00:09:44.810 align:middle line:84%
"find the book"-- so I
won't draw the whole thing,

00:09:44.810 --> 00:09:46.818 align:middle line:84%
but "find the book"--
there's this thing

00:09:46.818 --> 00:09:47.735 align:middle line:90%
and then there's this.

00:09:47.735 --> 00:09:51.350 align:middle line:84%
This is the VP because it's the
highest thing with the label V.

00:09:51.350 --> 00:09:53.990 align:middle line:84%
This is the V because it's
just the verb-- it's just

00:09:53.990 --> 00:09:55.250 align:middle line:90%
a word by itself.

00:09:55.250 --> 00:09:58.130 align:middle line:84%
And this thing, we use a
prime symbol to mark it,

00:09:58.130 --> 00:10:01.100 align:middle line:90%
and it is called a V bar.

00:10:01.100 --> 00:10:05.810 align:middle line:84%
I'm sorry, that's just the
way we do things in syntax.

00:10:05.810 --> 00:10:07.340 align:middle line:84%
I think it's called
this because it

00:10:07.340 --> 00:10:11.360 align:middle line:84%
used to be that people
would write it like this

00:10:11.360 --> 00:10:13.520 align:middle line:90%
with a bar over the top.

00:10:13.520 --> 00:10:15.290 align:middle line:90%
This was in like the--

00:10:15.290 --> 00:10:19.320 align:middle line:84%
I don't know-- the 1960s,
1970s people began doing this.

00:10:19.320 --> 00:10:21.650 align:middle line:84%
And I think people were
literally using typewriters

00:10:21.650 --> 00:10:25.520 align:middle line:84%
to go back and do an underscore
over the top of the V

00:10:25.520 --> 00:10:26.540 align:middle line:90%
or something like that.

00:10:26.540 --> 00:10:29.750 align:middle line:84%
I think that's what they were
doing with Stone Age word

00:10:29.750 --> 00:10:32.180 align:middle line:90%
processing technology.

00:10:32.180 --> 00:10:35.090 align:middle line:84%
So the result of all that is
that although nobody writes

00:10:35.090 --> 00:10:37.820 align:middle line:84%
V bar, typically,
you will sometimes

00:10:37.820 --> 00:10:40.060 align:middle line:84%
see this in textbooks
and things like that.

00:10:40.060 --> 00:10:41.090 align:middle line:90%
It's not very common.

00:10:41.090 --> 00:10:44.210 align:middle line:84%
What people much more commonly
do is just write V prime,

00:10:44.210 --> 00:10:46.430 align:middle line:84%
but it is never
ever called V prime.

00:10:46.430 --> 00:10:47.840 align:middle line:90%
It's always called V bar.

00:10:47.840 --> 00:10:51.135 align:middle line:84%
If you tell a syntactician,
"Find me the V prime node,"

00:10:51.135 --> 00:10:53.510 align:middle line:84%
the syntactician will not know
what you're talking about.

00:10:53.510 --> 00:10:54.590 align:middle line:90%
Sorry.

00:10:54.590 --> 00:10:55.280 align:middle line:90%
Yeah?

00:10:55.280 --> 00:10:58.565 align:middle line:84%
AUDIENCE: So if "find the
book" itself will not, like,

00:10:58.565 --> 00:11:00.545 align:middle line:84%
considered a verb
phrase, I guess,

00:11:00.545 --> 00:11:01.988 align:middle line:90%
would it have the bar or?

00:11:01.988 --> 00:11:03.530 align:middle line:84%
NORVIN RICHARDS: If
"find the book"--

00:11:03.530 --> 00:11:04.415 align:middle line:90%
in this tree?

00:11:04.415 --> 00:11:05.040 align:middle line:90%
AUDIENCE: Yeah.

00:11:05.040 --> 00:11:07.910 align:middle line:90%


00:11:07.910 --> 00:11:09.470 align:middle line:90%
NORVIN RICHARDS: So it's a--

00:11:09.470 --> 00:11:10.820 align:middle line:84%
I'm sorry, can you ask
your question again.

00:11:10.820 --> 00:11:12.528 align:middle line:84%
AUDIENCE: Yeah, because
I'm just thinking

00:11:12.528 --> 00:11:15.080 align:middle line:84%
from grammatical standpoint,
I guess, if "find the book"

00:11:15.080 --> 00:11:17.450 align:middle line:84%
would be considered
a verb phrase.

00:11:17.450 --> 00:11:20.010 align:middle line:84%
But since it's not the
highest standard of the bar,

00:11:20.010 --> 00:11:22.790 align:middle line:84%
but if it were
not a verb phrase,

00:11:22.790 --> 00:11:24.550 align:middle line:90%
would it still not apply?

00:11:24.550 --> 00:11:26.460 align:middle line:84%
NORVIN RICHARDS:
So if this sentence

00:11:26.460 --> 00:11:28.560 align:middle line:84%
ended with "find the
book"-- if it were "I

00:11:28.560 --> 00:11:30.720 align:middle line:84%
will find the book,"
then "find the book"

00:11:30.720 --> 00:11:34.035 align:middle line:90%
would be a verb phrase.

00:11:34.035 --> 00:11:36.720 align:middle line:84%
A phrase is just the
name for the highest

00:11:36.720 --> 00:11:38.620 align:middle line:90%
thing with that label.

00:11:38.620 --> 00:11:44.260 align:middle line:90%
So just like in "the book"--

00:11:44.260 --> 00:11:47.350 align:middle line:90%
yeah, the book is a noun phrase.

00:11:47.350 --> 00:11:50.500 align:middle line:90%
If you had something--

00:11:50.500 --> 00:11:53.110 align:middle line:84%
and "book" is not a noun
phrase, it's just an N.

00:11:53.110 --> 00:11:55.720 align:middle line:84%
So there are places
where you don't

00:11:55.720 --> 00:11:57.520 align:middle line:84%
get a bar just
because you only have

00:11:57.520 --> 00:11:58.990 align:middle line:90%
two things with that label.

00:11:58.990 --> 00:12:02.705 align:middle line:84%
And you just get the N,
and then there's the NP.

00:12:02.705 --> 00:12:04.330 align:middle line:84%
In this particular
case, you're getting

00:12:04.330 --> 00:12:07.990 align:middle line:84%
a bar because there are three
things with the label V.

00:12:07.990 --> 00:12:09.850 align:middle line:90%
And if there were more things--

00:12:09.850 --> 00:12:11.680 align:middle line:84%
if this verb phrase
were more complicated,

00:12:11.680 --> 00:12:13.480 align:middle line:84%
you could end up
with a bunch of bars

00:12:13.480 --> 00:12:15.190 align:middle line:90%
in between the verb and the VP.

00:12:15.190 --> 00:12:17.860 align:middle line:84%
In general, you're
going to get one P up

00:12:17.860 --> 00:12:20.950 align:middle line:84%
at the top and one thing
with no extra doohickey

00:12:20.950 --> 00:12:24.150 align:middle line:84%
down at the bottom, so just V.
And everything in between it

00:12:24.150 --> 00:12:26.590 align:middle line:90%
will be bar.

00:12:26.590 --> 00:12:28.090 align:middle line:84%
Again, this is all
just conventions.

00:12:28.090 --> 00:12:29.882 align:middle line:84%
This is not meant to
reflect anything deep.

00:12:29.882 --> 00:12:31.900 align:middle line:84%
It's meant to make it
possible for human beings

00:12:31.900 --> 00:12:33.580 align:middle line:84%
to look at these
trees and process

00:12:33.580 --> 00:12:34.690 align:middle line:90%
the information in them.

00:12:34.690 --> 00:12:37.950 align:middle line:90%
That's all it's for.

00:12:37.950 --> 00:12:39.210 align:middle line:90%
Raquel?

00:12:39.210 --> 00:12:43.290 align:middle line:84%
AUDIENCE: For things like "will
find" or like an auxiliary

00:12:43.290 --> 00:12:45.155 align:middle line:84%
where you squish
multiple verbs together,

00:12:45.155 --> 00:12:47.280 align:middle line:84%
is there something more
complicated going on there,

00:12:47.280 --> 00:12:50.130 align:middle line:84%
or could we say that they're
both part of a verb phrase?

00:12:50.130 --> 00:12:51.870 align:middle line:84%
NORVIN RICHARDS: We have not
gotten there, but you're right.

00:12:51.870 --> 00:12:54.720 align:middle line:84%
And so Raquel wants to know what
am I going to do with "I will"?

00:12:54.720 --> 00:12:56.160 align:middle line:84%
And I will-- I
will tell you what

00:12:56.160 --> 00:12:57.760 align:middle line:90%
I'm going to do with that now.

00:12:57.760 --> 00:13:01.220 align:middle line:84%
We will give "will" its
own label in just a second.

00:13:01.220 --> 00:13:02.730 align:middle line:90%
Good question.

00:13:02.730 --> 00:13:05.680 align:middle line:90%
Other questions about this?

00:13:05.680 --> 00:13:07.590 align:middle line:90%
Reasonably clear?

00:13:07.590 --> 00:13:12.330 align:middle line:90%
So-- but yeah.

00:13:12.330 --> 00:13:15.420 align:middle line:84%
So again, P is the marker for
the highest thing with a given

00:13:15.420 --> 00:13:16.458 align:middle line:90%
label.

00:13:16.458 --> 00:13:18.750 align:middle line:84%
The lowest thing with a given
label doesn't get a mark.

00:13:18.750 --> 00:13:22.260 align:middle line:84%
Sometimes, when people want
to make it clear that it's not

00:13:22.260 --> 00:13:25.350 align:middle line:84%
a bar and it's not a P, they'll
put a little raised 0 on it.

00:13:25.350 --> 00:13:30.000 align:middle line:84%
I won't do that, but you'll
see people do that sometimes.

00:13:30.000 --> 00:13:32.650 align:middle line:84%
So you've got the unmarked
thing-- that's the word itself.

00:13:32.650 --> 00:13:34.483 align:middle line:84%
You've got the P--
that's the highest thing,

00:13:34.483 --> 00:13:35.820 align:middle line:90%
and everything else is a bar.

00:13:35.820 --> 00:13:37.320 align:middle line:84%
One consequence of
all this actually

00:13:37.320 --> 00:13:45.275 align:middle line:84%
is that there are things which
are both phrases and heads.

00:13:45.275 --> 00:13:48.510 align:middle line:84%
This is connected
to Kirai's question

00:13:48.510 --> 00:13:49.780 align:middle line:90%
from a little while ago.

00:13:49.780 --> 00:13:54.870 align:middle line:84%
So the D "the" in this-- both
of the instances of D "the"

00:13:54.870 --> 00:13:58.320 align:middle line:84%
in this tree, they are the
lowest thing with the label D,

00:13:58.320 --> 00:13:59.970 align:middle line:90%
so they don't get marked.

00:13:59.970 --> 00:14:02.550 align:middle line:84%
They're also the highest
thing with the label D,

00:14:02.550 --> 00:14:05.770 align:middle line:84%
because neither of them
is higher than the other.

00:14:05.770 --> 00:14:07.890 align:middle line:90%
They're just two instances of D.

00:14:07.890 --> 00:14:12.330 align:middle line:84%
And so an influential school
of thought about what's

00:14:12.330 --> 00:14:13.930 align:middle line:84%
going on in cases
like that is to say,

00:14:13.930 --> 00:14:16.860 align:middle line:84%
yeah, those are both
phrases and not phrases.

00:14:16.860 --> 00:14:19.202 align:middle line:84%
Try not to be too uptight
about these labels.

00:14:19.202 --> 00:14:20.910 align:middle line:84%
That's one way to talk
about them anyway.

00:14:20.910 --> 00:14:24.480 align:middle line:90%


00:14:24.480 --> 00:14:31.340 align:middle line:90%
So now, I drew you a tree--

00:14:31.340 --> 00:14:34.772 align:middle line:84%
and some of you
last time objected.

00:14:34.772 --> 00:14:36.980 align:middle line:84%
I drew you a tree in which
a prepositional phrase was

00:14:36.980 --> 00:14:39.380 align:middle line:90%
modifying a verb phrase.

00:14:39.380 --> 00:14:41.540 align:middle line:84%
That's-- I'll show
you the tree again.

00:14:41.540 --> 00:14:43.940 align:middle line:84%
You've got a prepositional
phrase, "in the garage."

00:14:43.940 --> 00:14:48.710 align:middle line:84%
And I've got that prepositional
phrase merged together

00:14:48.710 --> 00:14:50.600 align:middle line:84%
with something with
the label V. That's

00:14:50.600 --> 00:14:52.460 align:middle line:84%
a prepositional phrase
that's telling you

00:14:52.460 --> 00:14:54.668 align:middle line:84%
where the finding of the
book is going to take place.

00:14:54.668 --> 00:14:57.920 align:middle line:84%
It's going to take
place in the garage.

00:14:57.920 --> 00:15:00.680 align:middle line:84%
Now, prepositional phrases
can also modify noun phrases.

00:15:00.680 --> 00:15:02.810 align:middle line:84%
You can say things
like "I will find books

00:15:02.810 --> 00:15:05.497 align:middle line:84%
about syntax," where "about
syntax" is telling you

00:15:05.497 --> 00:15:07.205 align:middle line:84%
what kinds of books
you're going to find.

00:15:07.205 --> 00:15:10.430 align:middle line:90%


00:15:10.430 --> 00:15:12.500 align:middle line:84%
Now, this is just
some consequences

00:15:12.500 --> 00:15:15.590 align:middle line:90%
of that we can think about.

00:15:15.590 --> 00:15:18.530 align:middle line:84%
First, we've said
that noun phrases can

00:15:18.530 --> 00:15:20.300 align:middle line:90%
contain prepositional phrases--

00:15:20.300 --> 00:15:22.587 align:middle line:90%
books about syntax.

00:15:22.587 --> 00:15:24.170 align:middle line:84%
And we know that
prepositional phrases

00:15:24.170 --> 00:15:27.530 align:middle line:84%
can contain noun
phrases, which can

00:15:27.530 --> 00:15:31.530 align:middle line:84%
contain prepositional phrases,
which can contain noun phrases.

00:15:31.530 --> 00:15:33.950 align:middle line:84%
So there is no reason
to ever stop talking.

00:15:33.950 --> 00:15:36.450 align:middle line:84%
We talked about this when
we were talking about--

00:15:36.450 --> 00:15:38.870 align:middle line:90%
I'm wearing my mask again.

00:15:38.870 --> 00:15:42.085 align:middle line:84%
We talked about this when
we were talking about--

00:15:42.085 --> 00:15:44.210 align:middle line:84%
the competence / performance
distinction-- the fact

00:15:44.210 --> 00:15:48.290 align:middle line:84%
that we're idealizing the
actual kinds of sentences

00:15:48.290 --> 00:15:49.055 align:middle line:90%
that people say.

00:15:49.055 --> 00:15:52.650 align:middle line:90%


00:15:52.650 --> 00:15:55.320 align:middle line:90%
Here's another case of this.

00:15:55.320 --> 00:15:57.540 align:middle line:84%
So you can have a
book about islands,

00:15:57.540 --> 00:15:59.670 align:middle line:84%
and you can have a book
about islands on lakes,

00:15:59.670 --> 00:16:02.040 align:middle line:84%
you can have a book about
islands on lakes on islands,

00:16:02.040 --> 00:16:03.790 align:middle line:84%
you can have a book
about islands on lakes

00:16:03.790 --> 00:16:05.190 align:middle line:90%
on islands on lakes, and so on.

00:16:05.190 --> 00:16:07.410 align:middle line:84%
Eventually, you will
run out of things

00:16:07.410 --> 00:16:10.260 align:middle line:84%
that your book can be about
if you keep repeating this,

00:16:10.260 --> 00:16:11.865 align:middle line:84%
but that is a fact
about geography,

00:16:11.865 --> 00:16:14.700 align:middle line:84%
and this is not a
geography class.

00:16:14.700 --> 00:16:17.640 align:middle line:84%
There actually is a
website-- or used to be--

00:16:17.640 --> 00:16:20.250 align:middle line:84%
that listed the largest
island on a lake,

00:16:20.250 --> 00:16:22.170 align:middle line:84%
and the largest island
on a lake on an island,

00:16:22.170 --> 00:16:24.540 align:middle line:84%
and the largest island on a
lake on an island on a lake.

00:16:24.540 --> 00:16:25.290 align:middle line:90%
It's kind of cool.

00:16:25.290 --> 00:16:28.602 align:middle line:90%


00:16:28.602 --> 00:16:31.980 align:middle line:84%
There's a lot of stuff
going around Lake Taal

00:16:31.980 --> 00:16:34.860 align:middle line:90%
in the Philippines I remember.

00:16:34.860 --> 00:16:35.520 align:middle line:90%
Is this clear?

00:16:35.520 --> 00:16:36.450 align:middle line:90%
So there's another--

00:16:36.450 --> 00:16:37.890 align:middle line:84%
I just said there is
another instance where

00:16:37.890 --> 00:16:40.432 align:middle line:84%
we might want to care about the
difference between competence

00:16:40.432 --> 00:16:41.940 align:middle line:90%
and performance.

00:16:41.940 --> 00:16:44.280 align:middle line:84%
We've got a theory
that we're starting

00:16:44.280 --> 00:16:48.930 align:middle line:84%
to build about syntax that makes
it possible for noun phrases

00:16:48.930 --> 00:16:51.450 align:middle line:90%
to be arbitrarily long.

00:16:51.450 --> 00:16:55.290 align:middle line:84%
No one's ever going to utter an
arbitrarily long noun phrase.

00:16:55.290 --> 00:16:58.290 align:middle line:84%
Nobody's going to keep talking
as long as this grammar would

00:16:58.290 --> 00:17:00.570 align:middle line:84%
allow you to, but that's
a fact about, as I said,

00:17:00.570 --> 00:17:03.030 align:middle line:90%
geography and life.

00:17:03.030 --> 00:17:06.119 align:middle line:84%
People have better things
to do than to continue

00:17:06.119 --> 00:17:08.200 align:middle line:90%
repeating these things.

00:17:08.200 --> 00:17:12.450 align:middle line:84%
But it's clear we want a
grammar that allows this.

00:17:12.450 --> 00:17:15.000 align:middle line:84%
So yeah, you couldn't actually
utter an infinitely long noun

00:17:15.000 --> 00:17:16.667 align:middle line:84%
phrase, but we now
have a grammar that's

00:17:16.667 --> 00:17:19.780 align:middle line:90%
capable of producing one.

00:17:19.780 --> 00:17:22.599 align:middle line:84%
Another thing that it gets
us-- and this is the heart

00:17:22.599 --> 00:17:25.930 align:middle line:84%
of the reaction that I was
getting last time when I showed

00:17:25.930 --> 00:17:28.900 align:middle line:84%
you the tree that I wanted
to do for that sentence--

00:17:28.900 --> 00:17:30.830 align:middle line:84%
is that there are
cases of ambiguity.

00:17:30.830 --> 00:17:32.800 align:middle line:84%
So this is a classic
Marx brothers

00:17:32.800 --> 00:17:37.360 align:middle line:84%
line in Animal
Crackers, I think,

00:17:37.360 --> 00:17:41.110 align:middle line:84%
Groucho Marx says to someone,
"I once shot an elephant

00:17:41.110 --> 00:17:42.220 align:middle line:90%
in my pajamas."

00:17:42.220 --> 00:17:44.440 align:middle line:84%
And there's a
pause, and he says,

00:17:44.440 --> 00:17:48.130 align:middle line:84%
"How he got in my
pajamas, I'll never know."

00:17:48.130 --> 00:17:52.098 align:middle line:84%
So Groucho Marx here is
making use of the ambiguity

00:17:52.098 --> 00:17:53.140 align:middle line:90%
of the original sentence.

00:17:53.140 --> 00:17:56.590 align:middle line:84%
"I shot an elephant in my
pajamas" has two things

00:17:56.590 --> 00:17:59.230 align:middle line:84%
that it could, in
principle, mean.

00:17:59.230 --> 00:18:04.540 align:middle line:84%
It could mean I shot an
elephant while I was-- so here's

00:18:04.540 --> 00:18:05.245 align:middle line:90%
in my pajamas.

00:18:05.245 --> 00:18:08.110 align:middle line:90%


00:18:08.110 --> 00:18:10.300 align:middle line:84%
The prepositional
phrase in my pajamas

00:18:10.300 --> 00:18:11.633 align:middle line:90%
could modify the elephant.

00:18:11.633 --> 00:18:13.300 align:middle line:84%
That is, it could be
it was the elephant

00:18:13.300 --> 00:18:16.450 align:middle line:90%
who was wearing the pajamas--

00:18:16.450 --> 00:18:18.010 align:middle line:84%
I shot an elephant
in my pajamas.

00:18:18.010 --> 00:18:22.300 align:middle line:84%
Or it could be that we put
together "shoot an elephant,"

00:18:22.300 --> 00:18:25.960 align:middle line:84%
and then we merge "in
my pajamas" with that

00:18:25.960 --> 00:18:29.782 align:middle line:84%
so that "in my pajamas" is
modifying the way in which I

00:18:29.782 --> 00:18:30.490 align:middle line:90%
shot an elephant.

00:18:30.490 --> 00:18:33.315 align:middle line:84%
That is, I shot an elephant
while I was wearing my pajamas.

00:18:33.315 --> 00:18:35.180 align:middle line:84%
And so I shot an
elephant in my pajamas,

00:18:35.180 --> 00:18:36.350 align:middle line:90%
it's an ambiguous sentence.

00:18:36.350 --> 00:18:39.070 align:middle line:84%
It could mean either
I was wearing pajamas

00:18:39.070 --> 00:18:41.890 align:middle line:84%
or the elephant was
wearing pajamas.

00:18:41.890 --> 00:18:44.980 align:middle line:90%
Do people get that ambiguity?

00:18:44.980 --> 00:18:47.170 align:middle line:90%
It's a classic joke.

00:18:47.170 --> 00:18:51.700 align:middle line:90%
It's odd that we enjoy this.

00:18:51.700 --> 00:18:56.230 align:middle line:84%
There's a lot of humor that has
this shape, where someone says

00:18:56.230 --> 00:18:59.290 align:middle line:84%
an ambiguous sentence that
has one normal meaning and one

00:18:59.290 --> 00:19:02.140 align:middle line:84%
strange meaning, and
then the punchline

00:19:02.140 --> 00:19:04.630 align:middle line:84%
reveals that they meant
the strange meaning.

00:19:04.630 --> 00:19:05.860 align:middle line:90%
That's the form of this joke.

00:19:05.860 --> 00:19:07.360 align:middle line:84%
"I shot an elephant
in my pajamas"--

00:19:07.360 --> 00:19:08.680 align:middle line:84%
you, of course,
think that he means

00:19:08.680 --> 00:19:09.910 align:middle line:90%
he was wearing his pajamas.

00:19:09.910 --> 00:19:11.410 align:middle line:84%
And then he reveals
that, no, it was

00:19:11.410 --> 00:19:13.160 align:middle line:84%
the elephant that was
wearing the pajamas,

00:19:13.160 --> 00:19:14.320 align:middle line:90%
and then everybody laughs.

00:19:14.320 --> 00:19:15.730 align:middle line:90%
As I said, it's odd that we--

00:19:15.730 --> 00:19:19.210 align:middle line:84%
I mean, normally, being told,
haha, you misunderstood me,

00:19:19.210 --> 00:19:20.980 align:middle line:90%
that's not fun.

00:19:20.980 --> 00:19:24.070 align:middle line:84%
But we actually pay
people to do this.

00:19:24.070 --> 00:19:24.970 align:middle line:90%
It's kind of strange.

00:19:24.970 --> 00:19:25.930 align:middle line:90%
Yeah?

00:19:25.930 --> 00:19:29.750 align:middle line:84%
AUDIENCE: So it almost seems
like the one on the left

00:19:29.750 --> 00:19:33.240 align:middle line:90%
that's like started reading is--

00:19:33.240 --> 00:19:34.657 align:middle line:84%
almost seems like
a simpler tree--

00:19:34.657 --> 00:19:35.573 align:middle line:90%
NORVIN RICHARDS: Uhuh.

00:19:35.573 --> 00:19:36.150 align:middle line:90%
[LAUGHS]

00:19:36.150 --> 00:19:36.540 align:middle line:90%
Yeah.

00:19:36.540 --> 00:19:38.748 align:middle line:84%
AUDIENCE: --but compared to
the more complicated one,

00:19:38.748 --> 00:19:41.292 align:middle line:84%
is that some of the reason
why this is ambiguous?

00:19:41.292 --> 00:19:42.750 align:middle line:84%
The one that has
the normal meaning

00:19:42.750 --> 00:19:44.850 align:middle line:84%
has this complicated
structure, but the one

00:19:44.850 --> 00:19:49.080 align:middle line:84%
that has the wrong meaning
is a very simple structure

00:19:49.080 --> 00:19:49.903 align:middle line:90%
[INAUDIBLE]?

00:19:49.903 --> 00:19:50.820 align:middle line:90%
NORVIN RICHARDS: Yeah.

00:19:50.820 --> 00:19:52.950 align:middle line:84%
No, it's interesting
that you have this--

00:19:52.950 --> 00:19:55.290 align:middle line:84%
I mean, right now,
what we have is

00:19:55.290 --> 00:19:57.390 align:middle line:84%
just a grammar that
predicts that it could

00:19:57.390 --> 00:19:59.130 align:middle line:90%
mean either of those things.

00:19:59.130 --> 00:20:01.950 align:middle line:84%
So that prepositional
phrase in my pajamas

00:20:01.950 --> 00:20:04.320 align:middle line:84%
can be merged in a couple
of different places,

00:20:04.320 --> 00:20:06.542 align:middle line:84%
and the consequence
is that it could mean

00:20:06.542 --> 00:20:07.750 align:middle line:90%
a couple of different things.

00:20:07.750 --> 00:20:09.792 align:middle line:84%
You're absolutely right
that the tree on the left

00:20:09.792 --> 00:20:12.060 align:middle line:84%
is prettier than the
tree on the right.

00:20:12.060 --> 00:20:18.630 align:middle line:84%
And people who work on how
people deal with ambiguities

00:20:18.630 --> 00:20:21.380 align:middle line:84%
like this in real-time-- this
is something people work on--

00:20:21.380 --> 00:20:26.850 align:middle line:84%
processing-- develop theories
about where the preferences are

00:20:26.850 --> 00:20:29.460 align:middle line:84%
for where prepositional
phrases should be attached

00:20:29.460 --> 00:20:32.843 align:middle line:84%
in a place like this, whether
there's a preference for trees

00:20:32.843 --> 00:20:34.260 align:middle line:84%
like the tree on
the left or trees

00:20:34.260 --> 00:20:35.430 align:middle line:90%
like the tree on the right.

00:20:35.430 --> 00:20:36.960 align:middle line:84%
I think there isn't
supposed to be

00:20:36.960 --> 00:20:42.702 align:middle line:84%
a general preference for trees
like the tree on the left.

00:20:42.702 --> 00:20:44.160 align:middle line:84%
I think this is
just supposed to be

00:20:44.160 --> 00:20:47.880 align:middle line:84%
a case where, in principle,
it's ambiguous and--

00:20:47.880 --> 00:20:49.553 align:middle line:84%
but people who
work on processing

00:20:49.553 --> 00:20:51.720 align:middle line:84%
have found places where
there's a preference for one

00:20:51.720 --> 00:20:53.970 align:middle line:84%
kind of tree over another,
so that is something people

00:20:53.970 --> 00:20:55.270 align:middle line:90%
work on.

00:20:55.270 --> 00:20:56.536 align:middle line:90%
Raquel?

00:20:56.536 --> 00:20:59.080 align:middle line:84%
AUDIENCE: I guess
a simple question--

00:20:59.080 --> 00:21:02.780 align:middle line:84%
so at the bottom of the left,
there's an NP, like my pajamas,

00:21:02.780 --> 00:21:06.350 align:middle line:90%
and there's N-bar that's higher.

00:21:06.350 --> 00:21:10.250 align:middle line:84%
I guess why isn't
an NP down there

00:21:10.250 --> 00:21:12.220 align:middle line:90%
if there's another NP up above?

00:21:12.220 --> 00:21:14.500 align:middle line:84%
NORVIN RICHARDS: Yeah, so
that's a very good point.

00:21:14.500 --> 00:21:19.030 align:middle line:84%
When I said before that you
give P to the highest thing with

00:21:19.030 --> 00:21:21.880 align:middle line:84%
a given label, I should
have said something more

00:21:21.880 --> 00:21:27.400 align:middle line:84%
sophisticated-- something like
you give P to the highest thing

00:21:27.400 --> 00:21:31.090 align:middle line:84%
with a given label that is
produced or that is being

00:21:31.090 --> 00:21:33.550 align:middle line:90%
projected from a given head--

00:21:33.550 --> 00:21:35.570 align:middle line:90%
from a given word.

00:21:35.570 --> 00:21:40.270 align:middle line:84%
So this-- so "my
pajamas" is an NP

00:21:40.270 --> 00:21:43.150 align:middle line:84%
because it's the highest node
that has an N that comes from

00:21:43.150 --> 00:21:45.100 align:middle line:90%
"pajamas."

00:21:45.100 --> 00:21:50.860 align:middle line:84%
And then there's a higher NP
that's right under the VP right

00:21:50.860 --> 00:21:52.780 align:middle line:84%
that you merge
together with the verb,

00:21:52.780 --> 00:21:56.710 align:middle line:84%
and that's an NP that's got
its label from "elephant."

00:21:56.710 --> 00:21:58.692 align:middle line:84%
So there are two NPs
here-- one of them

00:21:58.692 --> 00:22:01.150 align:middle line:84%
coming from "pajamas" and the
other coming from "elephant."

00:22:01.150 --> 00:22:02.200 align:middle line:90%
That's a very good point.

00:22:02.200 --> 00:22:03.742 align:middle line:84%
What you're pointing
out is that when

00:22:03.742 --> 00:22:06.310 align:middle line:84%
I said the highest
thing with the label N,

00:22:06.310 --> 00:22:07.570 align:middle line:90%
I was being too fast.

00:22:07.570 --> 00:22:09.640 align:middle line:84%
It's the highest thing
with a given label N,

00:22:09.640 --> 00:22:11.712 align:middle line:84%
and we have to distinguish
N's from each other.

00:22:11.712 --> 00:22:12.670 align:middle line:90%
Ooh, lots of questions.

00:22:12.670 --> 00:22:13.240 align:middle line:90%
Yes?

00:22:13.240 --> 00:22:15.040 align:middle line:84%
AUDIENCE: Actually,
I think the thing

00:22:15.040 --> 00:22:18.920 align:middle line:84%
about [INAUDIBLE] about the
[INAUDIBLE] simple are not

00:22:18.920 --> 00:22:19.840 align:middle line:90%
likely [INAUDIBLE].

00:22:19.840 --> 00:22:22.198 align:middle line:84%
Because I didn't get
the normal reading.

00:22:22.198 --> 00:22:23.240 align:middle line:90%
NORVIN RICHARDS: Oh, yes.

00:22:23.240 --> 00:22:23.740 align:middle line:90%
[LAUGHS]

00:22:23.740 --> 00:22:29.240 align:middle line:84%
AUDIENCE: [INAUDIBLE] imagining
this guy was shooting himself

00:22:29.240 --> 00:22:31.088 align:middle line:84%
while there was an
elephant [INAUDIBLE]..

00:22:31.088 --> 00:22:33.380 align:middle line:84%
NORVIN RICHARDS: Oh, the
elephant is inside his pajamas

00:22:33.380 --> 00:22:34.547 align:middle line:90%
and he is also wearing them.

00:22:34.547 --> 00:22:37.225 align:middle line:90%
Ah, yes.

00:22:37.225 --> 00:22:39.600 align:middle line:84%
I don't know what kind of tree
we want to build for that.

00:22:39.600 --> 00:22:40.892 align:middle line:90%
That's an interesting question.

00:22:40.892 --> 00:22:44.940 align:middle line:84%
Actually, we're going to
develop some tests in a second.

00:22:44.940 --> 00:22:48.110 align:middle line:84%
So far, all I've
done is assert this--

00:22:48.110 --> 00:22:49.790 align:middle line:90%
assert a couple of things.

00:22:49.790 --> 00:22:54.570 align:middle line:84%
That string of words ought to
have two different structures.

00:22:54.570 --> 00:22:56.030 align:middle line:84%
So that prepositional
phrase ought

00:22:56.030 --> 00:22:58.670 align:middle line:84%
to be able to modify either a
noun phrase or a verb phrase.

00:22:58.670 --> 00:23:01.970 align:middle line:84%
And we have the sense that that
string of words is ambiguous.

00:23:01.970 --> 00:23:05.000 align:middle line:84%
It can mean a couple
of different things.

00:23:05.000 --> 00:23:06.770 align:middle line:84%
I haven't yet given
you any reason

00:23:06.770 --> 00:23:09.350 align:middle line:84%
to believe that these
structures really

00:23:09.350 --> 00:23:12.410 align:middle line:84%
are the structures that are
associated with those meanings.

00:23:12.410 --> 00:23:15.320 align:middle line:84%
I'm going to try to do that in
a second to motivate that claim.

00:23:15.320 --> 00:23:17.790 align:middle line:84%
And let's bear your reading
in mind as I do that,

00:23:17.790 --> 00:23:20.120 align:middle line:84%
and we'll see how it behaves
with respect to the tests

00:23:20.120 --> 00:23:21.500 align:middle line:90%
that I'm going to show you.

00:23:21.500 --> 00:23:23.420 align:middle line:84%
This should all feel
kind of familiar, right?

00:23:23.420 --> 00:23:26.270 align:middle line:84%
So when we were doing
"unlockable," we were saying,

00:23:26.270 --> 00:23:28.040 align:middle line:84%
yeah, there's three
morphemes-- there are

00:23:28.040 --> 00:23:29.340 align:middle line:90%
two ways you can combine them.

00:23:29.340 --> 00:23:32.120 align:middle line:84%
And if we think about what these
morphemes do with the things

00:23:32.120 --> 00:23:35.180 align:middle line:84%
that they combine
with, we can understand

00:23:35.180 --> 00:23:37.310 align:middle line:84%
why the word "unlockable"
is ambiguous in the way

00:23:37.310 --> 00:23:38.330 align:middle line:90%
that it is.

00:23:38.330 --> 00:23:40.560 align:middle line:84%
We're doing something
similar here.

00:23:40.560 --> 00:23:43.910 align:middle line:84%
So our rules for
how things combine

00:23:43.910 --> 00:23:45.800 align:middle line:84%
allow us to create
these strings of words

00:23:45.800 --> 00:23:47.270 align:middle line:90%
in a couple of different ways.

00:23:47.270 --> 00:23:51.770 align:middle line:84%
And it's ambiguous-- wonder
if our freedom of building

00:23:51.770 --> 00:23:52.430 align:middle line:90%
couple--

00:23:52.430 --> 00:23:54.590 align:middle line:84%
having a couple of different
ways to build trees for this

00:23:54.590 --> 00:23:56.480 align:middle line:84%
has anything to do with
the fact that it seems

00:23:56.480 --> 00:23:57.710 align:middle line:90%
to mean two different things.

00:23:57.710 --> 00:23:59.720 align:middle line:90%
Joseph, did you have a question?

00:23:59.720 --> 00:24:00.560 align:middle line:90%
AUDIENCE: Yeah.

00:24:00.560 --> 00:24:03.775 align:middle line:84%
So looking at--
when you said that--

00:24:03.775 --> 00:24:05.150 align:middle line:84%
because on the
left tree, there's

00:24:05.150 --> 00:24:07.095 align:middle line:90%
two different noun phrases.

00:24:07.095 --> 00:24:11.520 align:middle line:84%
And so following up
on that, if you have--

00:24:11.520 --> 00:24:14.010 align:middle line:84%
would it also be
appropriate to say--

00:24:14.010 --> 00:24:18.300 align:middle line:84%
so "pajamas" down all
the way on the right

00:24:18.300 --> 00:24:19.620 align:middle line:90%
is part of a noun phrase--

00:24:19.620 --> 00:24:21.660 align:middle line:84%
and that's part of a
prepositional phrase,

00:24:21.660 --> 00:24:24.020 align:middle line:84%
so that kind of chain of the
noun phrase is terminated,

00:24:24.020 --> 00:24:26.220 align:middle line:84%
so it's OK to start
over with that?

00:24:26.220 --> 00:24:28.560 align:middle line:84%
NORVIN RICHARDS: That's--
yes, that would work fine too.

00:24:28.560 --> 00:24:32.640 align:middle line:84%
Your version of this
raises the question

00:24:32.640 --> 00:24:37.470 align:middle line:84%
of whether we'll ever find
a noun phrase that has--

00:24:37.470 --> 00:24:40.320 align:middle line:84%
that is merged together
with something else

00:24:40.320 --> 00:24:43.220 align:middle line:84%
that also has the label
N, or whether we'll ever

00:24:43.220 --> 00:24:44.970 align:middle line:84%
find a verb phrase
that is merged together

00:24:44.970 --> 00:24:47.430 align:middle line:84%
with something else that also
has the label V. I don't know

00:24:47.430 --> 00:24:49.230 align:middle line:84%
whether we'll get around
to seeing examples

00:24:49.230 --> 00:24:50.380 align:middle line:90%
like that in this class.

00:24:50.380 --> 00:24:52.260 align:middle line:84%
It's not clear that
there are such examples,

00:24:52.260 --> 00:24:54.900 align:middle line:84%
and if there aren't,
then that's interesting.

00:24:54.900 --> 00:24:57.100 align:middle line:84%
We might want to make
something of that.

00:24:57.100 --> 00:25:00.077 align:middle line:84%
So yes, your amendment
to what I said

00:25:00.077 --> 00:25:02.160 align:middle line:84%
and my amendment to what
I said are both good ways

00:25:02.160 --> 00:25:06.300 align:middle line:84%
of talking about the answer to
Raquel's question from earlier.

00:25:06.300 --> 00:25:07.176 align:middle line:90%
Yeah?

00:25:07.176 --> 00:25:08.650 align:middle line:84%
AUDIENCE: So just
to follow-up, I

00:25:08.650 --> 00:25:12.423 align:middle line:84%
know someone asked about "I
will," and so that, of course,

00:25:12.423 --> 00:25:14.840 align:middle line:90%
has a modifier.

00:25:14.840 --> 00:25:19.010 align:middle line:84%
But "I" in the sentence,
do we just not [INAUDIBLE]??

00:25:19.010 --> 00:25:20.010 align:middle line:90%
NORVIN RICHARDS: Oh, no.

00:25:20.010 --> 00:25:20.730 align:middle line:90%
See-- I'm sorry.

00:25:20.730 --> 00:25:22.147 align:middle line:84%
I'm being very
unfair to you guys.

00:25:22.147 --> 00:25:24.717 align:middle line:84%
I keep only showing you
trees for parts of sentences.

00:25:24.717 --> 00:25:26.550 align:middle line:84%
So we've not-- we haven't
gotten any further

00:25:26.550 --> 00:25:27.885 align:middle line:90%
than the verb phrase.

00:25:27.885 --> 00:25:29.010 align:middle line:90%
AUDIENCE: That [INAUDIBLE].

00:25:29.010 --> 00:25:31.830 align:middle line:84%
NORVIN RICHARDS: So I'm trying
to get as much mileage out

00:25:31.830 --> 00:25:34.170 align:middle line:84%
of the verb phrase as
I can before we go on

00:25:34.170 --> 00:25:35.367 align:middle line:90%
to larger things.

00:25:35.367 --> 00:25:37.950 align:middle line:84%
But you're right, eventually,
we will do sentences, I promise.

00:25:37.950 --> 00:25:40.800 align:middle line:90%


00:25:40.800 --> 00:25:42.960 align:middle line:90%
Questions about this?

00:25:42.960 --> 00:25:45.390 align:middle line:90%
They're all good.

00:25:45.390 --> 00:25:48.240 align:middle line:84%
I was saying to the TAs the
other day, you guys are--

00:25:48.240 --> 00:25:50.680 align:middle line:84%
it's nice being in a class
full of talkative people.

00:25:50.680 --> 00:25:53.370 align:middle line:90%
I appreciate it.

00:25:53.370 --> 00:25:56.967 align:middle line:84%
So two trees, and a hypothesis
that we might entertain

00:25:56.967 --> 00:25:59.550 align:middle line:84%
is that the tree on the left is
the one where the elephant was

00:25:59.550 --> 00:26:01.425 align:middle line:84%
wearing the pajamas and
the tree on the right

00:26:01.425 --> 00:26:05.220 align:middle line:84%
is the one where the
shooting involves

00:26:05.220 --> 00:26:07.206 align:middle line:90%
me being in the pajamas--

00:26:07.206 --> 00:26:09.330 align:middle line:90%
sort of the normal reading--

00:26:09.330 --> 00:26:10.830 align:middle line:84%
normal for some of
us, although it's

00:26:10.830 --> 00:26:12.788 align:middle line:84%
been established that
not all of us are normal.

00:26:12.788 --> 00:26:15.260 align:middle line:90%


00:26:15.260 --> 00:26:21.040 align:middle line:84%
Now, remember back when I
was trying to convince you

00:26:21.040 --> 00:26:23.500 align:middle line:84%
that there were such
things as constituents--

00:26:23.500 --> 00:26:31.090 align:middle line:84%
that syntax cared about whether
a particular string of words

00:26:31.090 --> 00:26:36.130 align:middle line:84%
were all descended from
a single node or not.

00:26:36.130 --> 00:26:39.130 align:middle line:84%
I was showing you tests
for constituent structure,

00:26:39.130 --> 00:26:41.650 align:middle line:84%
and in your recitation
sessions, you

00:26:41.650 --> 00:26:43.900 align:middle line:84%
may have played around
with different tests

00:26:43.900 --> 00:26:45.460 align:middle line:90%
for constituent structure.

00:26:45.460 --> 00:26:48.880 align:middle line:84%
So one of the tests that
we were fooling around with

00:26:48.880 --> 00:26:52.920 align:middle line:84%
was what I called
topicalization.

00:26:52.920 --> 00:26:56.170 align:middle line:84%
It's possible to
emphasize something

00:26:56.170 --> 00:26:59.750 align:middle line:84%
by moving it to the
beginning of the sentence.

00:26:59.750 --> 00:27:04.330 align:middle line:84%
So you can say things like "the
elephant I shot in my pajamas,"

00:27:04.330 --> 00:27:09.160 align:middle line:84%
or "the elephant in
my pajamas I shot."

00:27:09.160 --> 00:27:13.825 align:middle line:84%
Now, let's consider
the tree on the left--

00:27:13.825 --> 00:27:16.870 align:middle line:90%


00:27:16.870 --> 00:27:17.560 align:middle line:90%
this tree.

00:27:17.560 --> 00:27:20.530 align:middle line:84%
Is there a constituent
in that tree--

00:27:20.530 --> 00:27:27.120 align:middle line:84%
an "elephant" that doesn't
have anything else in it?

00:27:27.120 --> 00:27:30.240 align:middle line:84%
Several of you are appropriately
shaking your heads.

00:27:30.240 --> 00:27:37.927 align:middle line:84%
There are nodes that are
above the words "an elephant."

00:27:37.927 --> 00:27:39.510 align:middle line:84%
There's a D that's
above "in," there's

00:27:39.510 --> 00:27:40.770 align:middle line:90%
an N that's above "elephant."

00:27:40.770 --> 00:27:44.730 align:middle line:84%
And there's an NP
up there which has

00:27:44.730 --> 00:27:48.760 align:middle line:84%
"an elephant" as part of it,
but it also has "in my pajamas."

00:27:48.760 --> 00:27:50.310 align:middle line:84%
So in the tree on
the left, there's

00:27:50.310 --> 00:27:53.080 align:middle line:90%
no constituent "an elephant."

00:27:53.080 --> 00:27:54.580 align:middle line:84%
How about in the
tree on the right--

00:27:54.580 --> 00:27:57.010 align:middle line:84%
is there a constituent
"an elephant"?

00:27:57.010 --> 00:27:59.390 align:middle line:84%
Yeah, there's a noun
phrase like that.

00:27:59.390 --> 00:28:01.190 align:middle line:84%
So when we do the
topicalization--

00:28:01.190 --> 00:28:03.460 align:middle line:84%
"the elephant I shot
in my pajamas"--

00:28:03.460 --> 00:28:06.130 align:middle line:84%
we should only be able to do
the tree on the right and not

00:28:06.130 --> 00:28:07.730 align:middle line:90%
the tree on the left.

00:28:07.730 --> 00:28:09.670 align:middle line:84%
That is, it should
only have the meaning

00:28:09.670 --> 00:28:11.770 align:middle line:90%
that the tree on the right has--

00:28:11.770 --> 00:28:15.042 align:middle line:84%
that I was wearing the
pajamas and not the elephant.

00:28:15.042 --> 00:28:16.750 align:middle line:84%
And several of you
are raising your hands

00:28:16.750 --> 00:28:20.690 align:middle line:84%
and I'll call on you in just a
second, but before I do that,

00:28:20.690 --> 00:28:23.690 align:middle line:84%
do people have the
feeling that that's true?

00:28:23.690 --> 00:28:26.690 align:middle line:84%
That if I say "The elephant
I shot in my pajamas--

00:28:26.690 --> 00:28:30.890 align:middle line:84%
the aardvark I
shot in my tuxedo,"

00:28:30.890 --> 00:28:32.330 align:middle line:90%
that I'm wearing these things.

00:28:32.330 --> 00:28:34.850 align:middle line:90%
It's not my victims.

00:28:34.850 --> 00:28:36.470 align:middle line:90%
And I think that's right.

00:28:36.470 --> 00:28:39.140 align:middle line:84%
So the ambiguity that
we have if we haven't

00:28:39.140 --> 00:28:40.880 align:middle line:90%
done any topicalization--

00:28:40.880 --> 00:28:43.650 align:middle line:84%
that ambiguity was there because
we had both of these trees.

00:28:43.650 --> 00:28:46.550 align:middle line:84%
And if we run a
constituency test,

00:28:46.550 --> 00:28:49.788 align:middle line:84%
that collapses the ambiguity,
because it makes it

00:28:49.788 --> 00:28:51.830 align:middle line:84%
so that we could only have
the tree on the right,

00:28:51.830 --> 00:28:52.770 align:middle line:90%
not the tree on the left.

00:28:52.770 --> 00:28:54.770 align:middle line:84%
And so we can only have
the meaning on the right

00:28:54.770 --> 00:28:56.510 align:middle line:90%
and not the meaning on the left.

00:28:56.510 --> 00:28:57.560 align:middle line:90%
Yes?

00:28:57.560 --> 00:28:59.878 align:middle line:84%
Sorry, I'll get to
you in just a second.

00:28:59.878 --> 00:29:01.670 align:middle line:84%
AUDIENCE: So yeah, on
the tree on the left,

00:29:01.670 --> 00:29:04.610 align:middle line:84%
would it be more correct to
combine "an elephant" first

00:29:04.610 --> 00:29:07.810 align:middle line:84%
before adding it to
the big upper tree?

00:29:07.810 --> 00:29:10.480 align:middle line:84%
NORVIN RICHARDS: Oh, you mean
combine "an elephant" first

00:29:10.480 --> 00:29:13.850 align:middle line:84%
and then put "in my
pajamas" together with that?

00:29:13.850 --> 00:29:16.420 align:middle line:90%
AUDIENCE: Yeah.

00:29:16.420 --> 00:29:18.850 align:middle line:84%
NORVIN RICHARDS: So
we could do that.

00:29:18.850 --> 00:29:24.850 align:middle line:84%
Notice that if we did
that, there would again

00:29:24.850 --> 00:29:27.800 align:middle line:90%
be a constituent "an elephant."

00:29:27.800 --> 00:29:30.280 align:middle line:84%
So we would lose the
result that we've

00:29:30.280 --> 00:29:33.280 align:middle line:84%
got here, which is that if
you front "the elephant"--

00:29:33.280 --> 00:29:35.867 align:middle line:84%
I'm sorry, I keep switching back
and forth between the and an.

00:29:35.867 --> 00:29:38.200 align:middle line:84%
I'll just ask you to believe
me that they're the same as

00:29:38.200 --> 00:29:41.170 align:middle line:90%
far as this is concerned.

00:29:41.170 --> 00:29:42.788 align:middle line:84%
But if we topicalize
"the elephant"--

00:29:42.788 --> 00:29:44.830 align:middle line:84%
if we move that to the
beginning of the sentence,

00:29:44.830 --> 00:29:46.570 align:middle line:84%
then we lose the
tree on the left.

00:29:46.570 --> 00:29:49.000 align:middle line:84%
So you're-- let me make
sure I'm understanding what

00:29:49.000 --> 00:29:49.640 align:middle line:90%
you're saying.

00:29:49.640 --> 00:29:52.140 align:middle line:84%
You're saying, wait-- what about
a tree that would look like

00:29:52.140 --> 00:29:53.290 align:middle line:90%
this--

00:29:53.290 --> 00:30:04.630 align:middle line:84%
shot, and then we've got a
noun phrase "an elephant,"

00:30:04.630 --> 00:30:07.120 align:middle line:84%
and then we've got a
prepositional phrase here

00:30:07.120 --> 00:30:11.350 align:middle line:84%
"in the (noun phrase),"
and then determiner "my,"

00:30:11.350 --> 00:30:14.920 align:middle line:90%
and then noun "pajamas."

00:30:14.920 --> 00:30:17.980 align:middle line:90%
You're imagining this tree.

00:30:17.980 --> 00:30:21.760 align:middle line:84%
And yes, notice, though,
that if we had that tree,

00:30:21.760 --> 00:30:25.660 align:middle line:84%
there would be a constituent
here-- "an elephant."

00:30:25.660 --> 00:30:27.460 align:middle line:84%
So now, there are
two ways we can go.

00:30:27.460 --> 00:30:30.340 align:middle line:84%
One would be to say
aha, we're learning

00:30:30.340 --> 00:30:34.090 align:middle line:84%
that although prepositional
phrase can merge

00:30:34.090 --> 00:30:38.200 align:middle line:90%
with a projection of a noun--

00:30:38.200 --> 00:30:39.700 align:middle line:90%
something with the label N--

00:30:39.700 --> 00:30:42.520 align:middle line:84%
it can't-- it has
to merge with N,

00:30:42.520 --> 00:30:46.330 align:middle line:84%
or maybe it has to merge
before you merge a D--

00:30:46.330 --> 00:30:47.830 align:middle line:84%
that there are rules
about the order

00:30:47.830 --> 00:30:49.270 align:middle line:90%
in which you merge things.

00:30:49.270 --> 00:30:51.880 align:middle line:84%
We're going to get a chance
to talk about things like that

00:30:51.880 --> 00:30:52.540 align:middle line:90%
soon--

00:30:52.540 --> 00:30:56.920 align:middle line:84%
other places where you
are so far-- let's see.

00:30:56.920 --> 00:31:01.390 align:middle line:84%
We're at the stage of syntax
where life is easy and free.

00:31:01.390 --> 00:31:04.360 align:middle line:84%
There's ambiguity-- you can
create trees however you want.

00:31:04.360 --> 00:31:08.770 align:middle line:84%
You've now come up with a tree
that we want to exclude somehow

00:31:08.770 --> 00:31:12.370 align:middle line:84%
in order to avoid the result
that first sentence would have

00:31:12.370 --> 00:31:14.950 align:middle line:90%
this tree as a possible tree.

00:31:14.950 --> 00:31:16.160 align:middle line:90%
We want to avoid that.

00:31:16.160 --> 00:31:17.440 align:middle line:84%
So there are two kinds
of things we could do.

00:31:17.440 --> 00:31:19.810 align:middle line:84%
One would be to say, no,
you may not draw this tree,

00:31:19.810 --> 00:31:22.270 align:middle line:84%
and maybe that would be
about the order in which you

00:31:22.270 --> 00:31:24.640 align:middle line:84%
can merge D and
prepositional phrases.

00:31:24.640 --> 00:31:26.470 align:middle line:84%
That would be one
thing we could do.

00:31:26.470 --> 00:31:27.887 align:middle line:84%
Another thing we
could do would be

00:31:27.887 --> 00:31:32.170 align:middle line:84%
to say, yeah, in order
to be topicalized,

00:31:32.170 --> 00:31:33.970 align:middle line:90%
you have to be a constituent.

00:31:33.970 --> 00:31:36.550 align:middle line:84%
But actually, there are some
constituents that cannot

00:31:36.550 --> 00:31:39.730 align:middle line:84%
topicalize-- that is,
it's not a bi-conditional.

00:31:39.730 --> 00:31:41.830 align:middle line:84%
That would be the
other move to make.

00:31:41.830 --> 00:31:45.910 align:middle line:84%
So this has been a very long
and elaborate version of "yes."

00:31:45.910 --> 00:31:48.970 align:middle line:84%
And so you're right-- this
is an imaginable tree,

00:31:48.970 --> 00:31:50.618 align:middle line:90%
and so we must do something.

00:31:50.618 --> 00:31:52.660 align:middle line:84%
So I showed you a couple
of things that we could.

00:31:52.660 --> 00:31:54.700 align:middle line:90%
Does that make sense at all?

00:31:54.700 --> 00:31:55.240 align:middle line:90%
OK?

00:31:55.240 --> 00:31:55.930 align:middle line:90%
Good.

00:31:55.930 --> 00:31:57.040 align:middle line:90%
Katrina?

00:31:57.040 --> 00:31:59.140 align:middle line:84%
AUDIENCE: I was also
going to bring up

00:31:59.140 --> 00:32:02.800 align:middle line:84%
that you could combine "an
elephant" before merging

00:32:02.800 --> 00:32:04.973 align:middle line:84%
to the prepositional
phrase, and so--

00:32:04.973 --> 00:32:06.140 align:middle line:90%
NORVIN RICHARDS: Good point.

00:32:06.140 --> 00:32:07.570 align:middle line:90%
And so I've answered.

00:32:07.570 --> 00:32:08.440 align:middle line:90%
Good-- excellent.

00:32:08.440 --> 00:32:09.040 align:middle line:90%
Good question.

00:32:09.040 --> 00:32:09.820 align:middle line:90%
Yes, Joseph?

00:32:09.820 --> 00:32:12.470 align:middle line:84%
AUDIENCE: This might be
getting ahead of this--

00:32:12.470 --> 00:32:14.750 align:middle line:84%
NORVIN RICHARDS:
Where we want to be?

00:32:14.750 --> 00:32:18.015 align:middle line:84%
AUDIENCE: --but
obviously, I [INAUDIBLE]..

00:32:18.015 --> 00:32:21.715 align:middle line:84%
So if you break that
constituent off "an elephant"

00:32:21.715 --> 00:32:27.490 align:middle line:84%
in the second tree corresponding
to the first [INAUDIBLE]..

00:32:27.490 --> 00:32:31.490 align:middle line:90%
Breaking off "an elephant"--

00:32:31.490 --> 00:32:34.850 align:middle line:84%
I don't know what the actual
topology of these trees are

00:32:34.850 --> 00:32:37.240 align:middle line:84%
and what the rules
are, but aren't you

00:32:37.240 --> 00:32:39.782 align:middle line:90%
crossing that over the I branch?

00:32:39.782 --> 00:32:41.990 align:middle line:84%
NORVIN RICHARDS: Why don't
you remember that question

00:32:41.990 --> 00:32:45.560 align:middle line:84%
and ask it again once we
have complete sentences?

00:32:45.560 --> 00:32:49.550 align:middle line:84%
I mean, so far, the only
condition on topicalization--

00:32:49.550 --> 00:32:51.073 align:middle line:84%
this operation
that puts something

00:32:51.073 --> 00:32:52.490 align:middle line:84%
at the beginning
of the sentence--

00:32:52.490 --> 00:32:55.280 align:middle line:84%
that I've offered you is that
the thing that you topicalize

00:32:55.280 --> 00:32:57.500 align:middle line:90%
has to be a constituent.

00:32:57.500 --> 00:32:59.750 align:middle line:84%
I haven't said anything
else about where--

00:32:59.750 --> 00:33:01.220 align:middle line:84%
like, I haven't
offered to draw you

00:33:01.220 --> 00:33:04.010 align:middle line:84%
a tree for "the elephant I shot
in my pajamas," for example.

00:33:04.010 --> 00:33:07.410 align:middle line:84%
Eventually, I will, but I'm
not going to do that yet.

00:33:07.410 --> 00:33:10.278 align:middle line:84%
I may not even do it today,
partly because eventually,

00:33:10.278 --> 00:33:12.320 align:middle line:84%
we have to get "I shot an
elephant in my pajamas"

00:33:12.320 --> 00:33:13.115 align:middle line:90%
just for an example.

00:33:13.115 --> 00:33:13.615 align:middle line:90%
[SNEEZING]

00:33:13.615 --> 00:33:14.480 align:middle line:90%
Bless you.

00:33:14.480 --> 00:33:17.420 align:middle line:90%
So we'll get that first.

00:33:17.420 --> 00:33:19.177 align:middle line:90%
Yes?

00:33:19.177 --> 00:33:21.010 align:middle line:84%
AUDIENCE: I know this
is kind of [INAUDIBLE]

00:33:21.010 --> 00:33:25.280 align:middle line:84%
but with the example of
"an elephant" [INAUDIBLE]

00:33:25.280 --> 00:33:29.150 align:middle line:84%
is that its own constituent
so that we could technically

00:33:29.150 --> 00:33:34.367 align:middle line:84%
be like, "the elephant,
comma, in my pajamas, comma"?

00:33:34.367 --> 00:33:35.450 align:middle line:90%
NORVIN RICHARDS: Oh, dear.

00:33:35.450 --> 00:33:37.610 align:middle line:84%
AUDIENCE: Like, separated
it if was [INAUDIBLE]..

00:33:37.610 --> 00:33:38.777 align:middle line:90%
NORVIN RICHARDS: If it was--

00:33:38.777 --> 00:33:40.820 align:middle line:84%
you mean if we were able
to draw trees like this?

00:33:40.820 --> 00:33:41.445 align:middle line:90%
AUDIENCE: Yeah.

00:33:41.445 --> 00:33:43.628 align:middle line:90%


00:33:43.628 --> 00:33:45.170 align:middle line:84%
NORVIN RICHARDS: I
shot an elephant--

00:33:45.170 --> 00:33:46.432 align:middle line:90%
AUDIENCE: Oh, wait.

00:33:46.432 --> 00:33:48.052 align:middle line:90%
Did we explain why couldn't--

00:33:48.052 --> 00:33:50.010 align:middle line:84%
NORVIN RICHARDS: Why we
had better not do this?

00:33:50.010 --> 00:33:53.872 align:middle line:90%
So we had two people point out--

00:33:53.872 --> 00:33:55.580 align:middle line:84%
one of them actually
got to point it out,

00:33:55.580 --> 00:33:57.320 align:middle line:84%
and then Kateryna
pointed out that she

00:33:57.320 --> 00:33:58.580 align:middle line:90%
was going to point it out--

00:33:58.580 --> 00:34:01.250 align:middle line:84%
that this is a tree
that you could imagine.

00:34:01.250 --> 00:34:03.810 align:middle line:84%
You could imagine being able
to build a tree like this.

00:34:03.810 --> 00:34:06.290 align:middle line:90%
And my reaction was, oh, dear--

00:34:06.290 --> 00:34:08.690 align:middle line:84%
we must do something
to stop that.

00:34:08.690 --> 00:34:11.748 align:middle line:84%
So here-- I'll mark this
tree with a frowny face

00:34:11.748 --> 00:34:12.290 align:middle line:90%
or something.

00:34:12.290 --> 00:34:15.650 align:middle line:84%
We've got to exclude
this tree somehow--

00:34:15.650 --> 00:34:16.969 align:middle line:90%
maybe.

00:34:16.969 --> 00:34:20.510 align:middle line:84%
We've got we've got to
avoid the following problem.

00:34:20.510 --> 00:34:22.699 align:middle line:84%
This sure looks like
a tree for a meaning

00:34:22.699 --> 00:34:24.650 align:middle line:84%
where the elephant is
wearing the pajamas.

00:34:24.650 --> 00:34:26.210 align:middle line:84%
So a tree that has
the same meaning

00:34:26.210 --> 00:34:27.949 align:middle line:90%
as the tree on the left.

00:34:27.949 --> 00:34:29.540 align:middle line:84%
But it's also a tree
in which there's

00:34:29.540 --> 00:34:31.429 align:middle line:90%
a constituent "an elephant."

00:34:31.429 --> 00:34:33.500 align:middle line:84%
And the fact that I
wanted to get across

00:34:33.500 --> 00:34:35.300 align:middle line:84%
to you with this
slide was that if you

00:34:35.300 --> 00:34:37.120 align:middle line:90%
use topicalization to make it--

00:34:37.120 --> 00:34:40.010 align:middle line:84%
to find out-- I was only going
to choose between these two

00:34:40.010 --> 00:34:40.550 align:middle line:90%
trees--

00:34:40.550 --> 00:34:43.920 align:middle line:84%
to find out whether an elephant
is a constituent or not,

00:34:43.920 --> 00:34:46.050 align:middle line:84%
then you have to have
the tree on the right

00:34:46.050 --> 00:34:47.300 align:middle line:90%
where I'm wearing the pajamas.

00:34:47.300 --> 00:34:49.333 align:middle line:84%
The elephant can't be
wearing the pajamas.

00:34:49.333 --> 00:34:50.750 align:middle line:84%
Two people immediately
said, wait,

00:34:50.750 --> 00:34:52.750 align:middle line:84%
what about this-- imagine
the frowning face tree

00:34:52.750 --> 00:34:54.770 align:middle line:90%
that I don't have on the slide.

00:34:54.770 --> 00:34:56.690 align:middle line:84%
And I said there are
two things we could do.

00:34:56.690 --> 00:35:01.160 align:middle line:84%
One would be to say, no, you may
not do this maybe because there

00:35:01.160 --> 00:35:04.580 align:middle line:84%
are rules-- which maybe someday,
we'll get to-- that constrain

00:35:04.580 --> 00:35:08.690 align:middle line:84%
the order in which you can merge
a prepositional phrase and a D

00:35:08.690 --> 00:35:10.010 align:middle line:90%
with a noun--

00:35:10.010 --> 00:35:12.920 align:middle line:84%
rules that will
guarantee that if you're

00:35:12.920 --> 00:35:15.230 align:middle line:84%
going to put a prepositional
phrase and also a D

00:35:15.230 --> 00:35:17.180 align:middle line:84%
in projections of
a noun, you better

00:35:17.180 --> 00:35:19.250 align:middle line:84%
do the prepositional phrase
first and then the D.

00:35:19.250 --> 00:35:21.650 align:middle line:84%
And then we get
to ask, well, why?

00:35:21.650 --> 00:35:22.980 align:middle line:90%
Where did that rule come from?

00:35:22.980 --> 00:35:25.105 align:middle line:84%
And the answer is, well,
this is day two of syntax.

00:35:25.105 --> 00:35:26.270 align:middle line:90%
But we'll get there.

00:35:26.270 --> 00:35:27.290 align:middle line:90%
AUDIENCE: [INAUDIBLE]

00:35:27.290 --> 00:35:29.040 align:middle line:84%
NORVIN RICHARDS: Did that--
no, no, no, that's fine.

00:35:29.040 --> 00:35:30.470 align:middle line:84%
The other thing we
could do-- and here,

00:35:30.470 --> 00:35:32.070 align:middle line:84%
let me say something
I said before,

00:35:32.070 --> 00:35:34.195 align:middle line:84%
but I'll say it a little
more coherently possibly--

00:35:34.195 --> 00:35:34.940 align:middle line:90%
we'll see--

00:35:34.940 --> 00:35:39.260 align:middle line:84%
would be to say in order
to topicalize-- in order

00:35:39.260 --> 00:35:41.330 align:middle line:84%
to be fronted-- you have
to be a constituent.

00:35:41.330 --> 00:35:43.250 align:middle line:84%
But it's not enough
to be a constituent.

00:35:43.250 --> 00:35:45.920 align:middle line:84%
Notice, the thing that
you would be fronting

00:35:45.920 --> 00:35:47.540 align:middle line:90%
in the tree on the right.

00:35:47.540 --> 00:35:51.437 align:middle line:84%
It's not just a constituent,
it's a noun phrase.

00:35:51.437 --> 00:35:54.020 align:middle line:84%
And what you would be fronting
over here is not a noun phrase,

00:35:54.020 --> 00:35:55.970 align:middle line:90%
it's an N bar.

00:35:55.970 --> 00:35:58.670 align:middle line:84%
So you could say, ah, we're
learning something new

00:35:58.670 --> 00:36:00.635 align:middle line:90%
about topicalization.

00:36:00.635 --> 00:36:03.453 align:middle line:84%
Topicalization-- yes, you
must be a constituent,

00:36:03.453 --> 00:36:05.120 align:middle line:84%
but it's not enough
to be a constituent.

00:36:05.120 --> 00:36:06.620 align:middle line:90%
You must actually be a phrase.

00:36:06.620 --> 00:36:08.540 align:middle line:90%
You cannot just be a bar.

00:36:08.540 --> 00:36:12.020 align:middle line:84%
And then that would be a new
and exciting thing to learn.

00:36:12.020 --> 00:36:13.940 align:middle line:84%
We have to find out
which of these ways

00:36:13.940 --> 00:36:16.068 align:middle line:84%
of dealing with this
tree is the better way.

00:36:16.068 --> 00:36:17.360 align:middle line:90%
You had a question a while ago.

00:36:17.360 --> 00:36:17.860 align:middle line:90%
I'm sorry.

00:36:17.860 --> 00:36:21.590 align:middle line:84%
AUDIENCE: Yes, so I might
be jumping the gun here,

00:36:21.590 --> 00:36:27.240 align:middle line:84%
but it almost seems to me
like in the first tree--

00:36:27.240 --> 00:36:28.100 align:middle line:90%
no, that's a lie.

00:36:28.100 --> 00:36:28.600 align:middle line:90%
Never mind.

00:36:28.600 --> 00:36:29.517 align:middle line:90%
NORVIN RICHARDS: Good.

00:36:29.517 --> 00:36:30.150 align:middle line:90%
[LAUGHTER]

00:36:30.150 --> 00:36:31.265 align:middle line:90%
Yes?

00:36:31.265 --> 00:36:33.390 align:middle line:84%
AUDIENCE: We're doing all
of this based on the fact

00:36:33.390 --> 00:36:36.630 align:middle line:84%
that we don't want the kind of
meaning where the elephant is

00:36:36.630 --> 00:36:39.450 align:middle line:84%
wearing your pajamas,
but what if you want

00:36:39.450 --> 00:36:42.002 align:middle line:90%
to say a sentence like that?

00:36:42.002 --> 00:36:42.960 align:middle line:90%
NORVIN RICHARDS: Well--

00:36:42.960 --> 00:36:45.060 align:middle line:84%
AUDIENCE: We're
trying to exclude

00:36:45.060 --> 00:36:46.792 align:middle line:84%
that meaning from the
sentence, but what

00:36:46.792 --> 00:36:48.000 align:middle line:90%
if someone wants to say that.

00:36:48.000 --> 00:36:48.890 align:middle line:90%
NORVIN RICHARDS: Well, so--

00:36:48.890 --> 00:36:50.890 align:middle line:84%
AUDIENCE: "I exterminated
the bugs in my walls."

00:36:50.890 --> 00:36:52.063 align:middle line:90%
NORVIN RICHARDS: So wait.

00:36:52.063 --> 00:36:53.480 align:middle line:84%
Leave the bugs
alone for a second.

00:36:53.480 --> 00:36:55.080 align:middle line:90%
[LAUGHTER]

00:36:55.080 --> 00:36:58.220 align:middle line:84%
We have-- there's enough
death in this class already.

00:36:58.220 --> 00:36:59.000 align:middle line:90%
Whoa, go back.

00:36:59.000 --> 00:37:02.480 align:middle line:90%


00:37:02.480 --> 00:37:04.610 align:middle line:90%
If somebody wants the meaning--

00:37:04.610 --> 00:37:05.318 align:middle line:90%
so let's back up.

00:37:05.318 --> 00:37:07.568 align:middle line:84%
Here's what I was going to
do with this pair of trees.

00:37:07.568 --> 00:37:09.950 align:middle line:84%
I was going to say, hey,
look, "I shot the elephant

00:37:09.950 --> 00:37:11.750 align:middle line:84%
in my pajamas" is
ambiguous in the way

00:37:11.750 --> 00:37:13.370 align:middle line:84%
that Groucho Marx
took advantage of,

00:37:13.370 --> 00:37:15.120 align:middle line:90%
and the ambiguity goes away.

00:37:15.120 --> 00:37:17.120 align:middle line:84%
And so far, we've only
done the first sentence--

00:37:17.120 --> 00:37:18.770 align:middle line:84%
"The elephant I
shot in my pajamas."

00:37:18.770 --> 00:37:20.390 align:middle line:84%
That has to be the
tree on the right.

00:37:20.390 --> 00:37:21.973 align:middle line:84%
The tree on the right
is the only tree

00:37:21.973 --> 00:37:23.312 align:middle line:90%
in which that's a constituent.

00:37:23.312 --> 00:37:25.520 align:middle line:84%
Immediately, people began
doing the frowny face tree.

00:37:25.520 --> 00:37:27.228 align:middle line:84%
Forget the frowny face
tree for a second.

00:37:27.228 --> 00:37:29.165 align:middle line:90%
Those are the only two trees.

00:37:29.165 --> 00:37:30.950 align:middle line:84%
It has to be the
tree on the right.

00:37:30.950 --> 00:37:36.600 align:middle line:84%
And "The elephant in
my pajamas I shot"--

00:37:36.600 --> 00:37:40.998 align:middle line:84%
the ambiguity also collapses,
but in the other direction.

00:37:40.998 --> 00:37:42.040 align:middle line:90%
People have that feeling?

00:37:42.040 --> 00:37:43.920 align:middle line:84%
So if I say, "The
elephant in my pajamas

00:37:43.920 --> 00:37:49.830 align:middle line:84%
I shot-- the elephant in my
tuxedo I offered some bananas."

00:37:49.830 --> 00:37:52.950 align:middle line:90%


00:37:52.950 --> 00:37:54.450 align:middle line:84%
That doesn't mean
I shot an elephant

00:37:54.450 --> 00:37:55.742 align:middle line:90%
while I was wearing my pajamas.

00:37:55.742 --> 00:37:58.080 align:middle line:84%
That second one-- "The elephant
in my pajamas I shot"--

00:37:58.080 --> 00:38:00.370 align:middle line:84%
only means that the elephant
was wearing the pajamas.

00:38:00.370 --> 00:38:00.930 align:middle line:90%
Why?

00:38:00.930 --> 00:38:03.120 align:middle line:84%
Well, if we look at the
trees we can see why.

00:38:03.120 --> 00:38:05.820 align:middle line:84%
There's a noun phrase-- "an
elephant in my pajamas"--

00:38:05.820 --> 00:38:07.620 align:middle line:84%
in the tree on the
left, and there's

00:38:07.620 --> 00:38:09.720 align:middle line:84%
no constituent "an
elephant in my pajamas"

00:38:09.720 --> 00:38:10.810 align:middle line:90%
in the tree on the right.

00:38:10.810 --> 00:38:13.260 align:middle line:90%
So these constituency tests--

00:38:13.260 --> 00:38:17.340 align:middle line:84%
topicalization-- forces us to
one or the other of those two

00:38:17.340 --> 00:38:18.060 align:middle line:90%
trees.

00:38:18.060 --> 00:38:20.955 align:middle line:84%
And depending on which
tree you're forced into,

00:38:20.955 --> 00:38:24.285 align:middle line:84%
you only have the reading that's
associated with that tree.

00:38:24.285 --> 00:38:27.280 align:middle line:84%
And so the ambiguity collapses
in the way that it should.

00:38:27.280 --> 00:38:29.710 align:middle line:84%
So once we nail down which
tree we're looking at,

00:38:29.710 --> 00:38:31.740 align:middle line:84%
we also nail down
which meaning we have.

00:38:31.740 --> 00:38:35.930 align:middle line:90%


00:38:35.930 --> 00:38:37.580 align:middle line:84%
Kateryna wanted to
know what if I'm

00:38:37.580 --> 00:38:39.290 align:middle line:84%
interested in the
tree on the left?

00:38:39.290 --> 00:38:42.990 align:middle line:84%
The answer is, well, either
don't topicalize or do

00:38:42.990 --> 00:38:44.990 align:middle line:84%
the topicalization-- the
second topicalization--

00:38:44.990 --> 00:38:46.820 align:middle line:84%
The elephant in my
pajamas I shot."

00:38:46.820 --> 00:38:52.160 align:middle line:84%
Those are both consistent
with the tree on the left,

00:38:52.160 --> 00:38:55.520 align:middle line:84%
but you can't do the
first topicalization.

00:38:55.520 --> 00:38:58.940 align:middle line:84%
Now, have I already
warned you about this?

00:38:58.940 --> 00:39:01.515 align:middle line:84%
There's a danger if you
go further in linguistics

00:39:01.515 --> 00:39:03.890 align:middle line:84%
that you will lose the ability
to distinguish grammatical

00:39:03.890 --> 00:39:05.270 align:middle line:90%
from ungrammatical sentences.

00:39:05.270 --> 00:39:07.520 align:middle line:84%
So your feelings about
sentences will all

00:39:07.520 --> 00:39:09.050 align:middle line:90%
become this kind of gray blur.

00:39:09.050 --> 00:39:11.210 align:middle line:84%
It's sometimes called
syntacticians' disease.

00:39:11.210 --> 00:39:15.470 align:middle line:84%
And I have been a syntactician
for longer than you have been

00:39:15.470 --> 00:39:17.690 align:middle line:90%
alive, which is depressing.

00:39:17.690 --> 00:39:20.000 align:middle line:84%
And so I no longer
have any judgments

00:39:20.000 --> 00:39:21.560 align:middle line:90%
at all about sentences.

00:39:21.560 --> 00:39:23.060 align:middle line:84%
But I think this
is how it works.

00:39:23.060 --> 00:39:24.950 align:middle line:84%
Does anybody want
to object when I

00:39:24.950 --> 00:39:27.140 align:middle line:84%
say these
topicalizations only have

00:39:27.140 --> 00:39:29.510 align:middle line:90%
one meaning and not the other?

00:39:29.510 --> 00:39:30.534 align:middle line:90%
Yeah?

00:39:30.534 --> 00:39:32.350 align:middle line:84%
AUDIENCE: So how
would you say that,

00:39:32.350 --> 00:39:37.160 align:middle line:84%
while this topicalization
[INAUDIBLE],,

00:39:37.160 --> 00:39:41.460 align:middle line:84%
and while [INAUDIBLE],,
it doesn't--

00:39:41.460 --> 00:39:45.676 align:middle line:84%
the first sentence still
points to the second tree

00:39:45.676 --> 00:39:48.690 align:middle line:90%
on the top slide.

00:39:48.690 --> 00:39:50.780 align:middle line:84%
NORVIN RICHARDS: To
the tree on the left?

00:39:50.780 --> 00:39:53.840 align:middle line:84%
AUDIENCE: Right, because
while they both have

00:39:53.840 --> 00:39:57.424 align:middle line:84%
the constituent "an
elephant," the difference

00:39:57.424 --> 00:40:00.310 align:middle line:90%
is that "shot" has a verb--

00:40:00.310 --> 00:40:04.700 align:middle line:84%
has a V-bar that's combined
with "in my pajamas"

00:40:04.700 --> 00:40:10.436 align:middle line:84%
while "shot" is never directly
combined with [INAUDIBLE]..

00:40:10.436 --> 00:40:12.623 align:middle line:84%
So I only point out
that there might

00:40:12.623 --> 00:40:17.726 align:middle line:84%
be a rule that only when they're
directly combined together,

00:40:17.726 --> 00:40:22.100 align:middle line:90%
they have [INAUDIBLE].

00:40:22.100 --> 00:40:24.890 align:middle line:84%
NORVIN RICHARDS: So I was
with you right up until--

00:40:24.890 --> 00:40:29.240 align:middle line:84%
I thought I was with you
right up until the end there.

00:40:29.240 --> 00:40:33.920 align:middle line:84%
The sentence, "I shot an
elephant in my pajamas,"

00:40:33.920 --> 00:40:38.120 align:middle line:84%
the verb phrase can have
either of these structures.

00:40:38.120 --> 00:40:39.125 align:middle line:90%
Is that-- no?

00:40:39.125 --> 00:40:41.034 align:middle line:84%
AUDIENCE: Because I
think the first sentence,

00:40:41.034 --> 00:40:43.500 align:middle line:90%
you said I shot in my pajamas.

00:40:43.500 --> 00:40:46.868 align:middle line:84%
So it was shot [INAUDIBLE]
pajamas [INAUDIBLE]..

00:40:46.868 --> 00:40:49.048 align:middle line:90%
NORVIN RICHARDS: Oh, I see.

00:40:49.048 --> 00:40:51.340 align:middle line:84%
And so you can only do that
with the tree on the right,

00:40:51.340 --> 00:40:53.200 align:middle line:84%
because "in my pajamas"
got merged together

00:40:53.200 --> 00:40:56.830 align:middle line:84%
with a projection of
the verb "shot," whereas

00:40:56.830 --> 00:40:58.840 align:middle line:90%
in the second one--

00:40:58.840 --> 00:41:00.595 align:middle line:84%
"The elephant in my
pajamas I shot"--

00:41:00.595 --> 00:41:03.370 align:middle line:90%


00:41:03.370 --> 00:41:08.294 align:middle line:84%
what's wrong with having
either of these trees?

00:41:08.294 --> 00:41:10.670 align:middle line:84%
AUDIENCE: So "The elephant
in my pajamas I shot"

00:41:10.670 --> 00:41:17.180 align:middle line:84%
couldn't mean [INAUDIBLE]
because an elephant is never

00:41:17.180 --> 00:41:19.770 align:middle line:90%
[INAUDIBLE].

00:41:19.770 --> 00:41:21.710 align:middle line:90%
NORVIN RICHARDS: Ah, I see.

00:41:21.710 --> 00:41:23.360 align:middle line:90%
Yes, I think--

00:41:23.360 --> 00:41:24.920 align:middle line:84%
I could be wrong,
but I think what

00:41:24.920 --> 00:41:29.420 align:middle line:84%
you are saying is another
way of what I am saying.

00:41:29.420 --> 00:41:31.960 align:middle line:84%
I think we're saying the
same thing in different ways.

00:41:31.960 --> 00:41:36.110 align:middle line:84%
So I am talking
about these trees

00:41:36.110 --> 00:41:40.460 align:middle line:84%
as though they are objects
that you can grab nodes in them

00:41:40.460 --> 00:41:41.990 align:middle line:90%
and move them around.

00:41:41.990 --> 00:41:43.520 align:middle line:84%
You are talking
about these trees

00:41:43.520 --> 00:41:46.700 align:middle line:84%
as if they are
sequences of events.

00:41:46.700 --> 00:41:49.430 align:middle line:84%
They're-- which is the way,
I encourage you to talk about

00:41:49.430 --> 00:41:50.270 align:middle line:90%
trees earlier on.

00:41:50.270 --> 00:41:52.910 align:middle line:84%
They're records of
the order in which you

00:41:52.910 --> 00:41:54.890 align:middle line:90%
put two things together.

00:41:54.890 --> 00:41:59.120 align:middle line:84%
And you're saying, yeah,
if you put "in my pajamas"

00:41:59.120 --> 00:42:03.030 align:middle line:84%
together with "shot," then
you can do the first sentence.

00:42:03.030 --> 00:42:04.700 align:middle line:84%
And if you put "in
my pajamas" together

00:42:04.700 --> 00:42:08.480 align:middle line:84%
with "the elephant," then you
can do the second sentence.

00:42:08.480 --> 00:42:11.690 align:middle line:84%
That is, which kinds of
things you can topicalize

00:42:11.690 --> 00:42:15.800 align:middle line:84%
cares about which order
you put things together in.

00:42:15.800 --> 00:42:18.810 align:middle line:84%
And I think we are
saying similar things.

00:42:18.810 --> 00:42:20.810 align:middle line:84%
I'm just saying it
representationally,

00:42:20.810 --> 00:42:21.350 align:middle line:90%
if you want.

00:42:21.350 --> 00:42:23.420 align:middle line:84%
I'm inviting you to
look at these trees

00:42:23.420 --> 00:42:25.820 align:middle line:84%
and think about them as objects
that you can move around,

00:42:25.820 --> 00:42:28.670 align:middle line:84%
and you're thinking in terms
of the order in which you

00:42:28.670 --> 00:42:30.210 align:middle line:90%
to put things together.

00:42:30.210 --> 00:42:33.050 align:middle line:84%
So I think you're right, but
you're right in the same way

00:42:33.050 --> 00:42:34.910 align:middle line:90%
that I am right.

00:42:34.910 --> 00:42:37.780 align:middle line:84%
This is the kind conclusion
of this kind of conversation

00:42:37.780 --> 00:42:40.280 align:middle line:84%
that I always like-- it's the
one where everyone involved is

00:42:40.280 --> 00:42:42.300 align:middle line:90%
right.

00:42:42.300 --> 00:42:46.140 align:middle line:84%
Other questions
about this so far?

00:42:46.140 --> 00:42:46.820 align:middle line:90%
OK.

00:42:46.820 --> 00:42:49.310 align:middle line:84%
So elephants-- is this
enough elephant violence?

00:42:49.310 --> 00:42:50.150 align:middle line:90%
Possibly.

00:42:50.150 --> 00:42:51.027 align:middle line:90%
Let's see.

00:42:51.027 --> 00:42:52.610 align:middle line:84%
Oh, no, there's so
much more violence.

00:42:52.610 --> 00:42:53.810 align:middle line:90%
I'm sorry.

00:42:53.810 --> 00:42:56.720 align:middle line:84%
So this is just
graphically illustrating

00:42:56.720 --> 00:42:58.160 align:middle line:90%
what I just showed you--

00:42:58.160 --> 00:43:01.400 align:middle line:84%
or told you in the
first sentence.

00:43:01.400 --> 00:43:03.470 align:middle line:84%
There needs to be a
constituent, the elephant.

00:43:03.470 --> 00:43:05.120 align:middle line:84%
And it's-- yeah, the
blue constituent.

00:43:05.120 --> 00:43:07.520 align:middle line:84%
It's the one in the
tree on the right.

00:43:07.520 --> 00:43:09.445 align:middle line:84%
And in the second
sentence, there

00:43:09.445 --> 00:43:10.820 align:middle line:84%
needs to be a
green constituent--

00:43:10.820 --> 00:43:12.612 align:middle line:84%
a constituent "the
elephant in my pajamas,"

00:43:12.612 --> 00:43:14.660 align:middle line:84%
and it's the constituent
in the tree on the left.

00:43:14.660 --> 00:43:18.510 align:middle line:90%


00:43:18.510 --> 00:43:22.080 align:middle line:84%
Notice that this also
makes predictions

00:43:22.080 --> 00:43:23.620 align:middle line:90%
about other kinds of sentences.

00:43:23.620 --> 00:43:29.670 align:middle line:84%
So if you had
thought these trees--

00:43:29.670 --> 00:43:30.865 align:middle line:90%
all this stuff about merge--

00:43:30.865 --> 00:43:32.490 align:middle line:84%
the order in which
you'd merge things--

00:43:32.490 --> 00:43:34.240 align:middle line:84%
you don't really
need any of this.

00:43:34.240 --> 00:43:37.230 align:middle line:84%
Here's an imaginable
thing you could think.

00:43:37.230 --> 00:43:40.680 align:middle line:84%
You would need to get us
to move fairly quickly

00:43:40.680 --> 00:43:41.820 align:middle line:90%
through that last slide.

00:43:41.820 --> 00:43:44.530 align:middle line:84%
But forgetting about
topicalization for a second,

00:43:44.530 --> 00:43:46.530 align:middle line:84%
if I told you, "Hey,
look, I shot an elephant

00:43:46.530 --> 00:43:48.540 align:middle line:84%
in my pajamas," it's
ambiguous as to who's

00:43:48.540 --> 00:43:50.040 align:middle line:90%
wearing the pajamas.

00:43:50.040 --> 00:43:52.645 align:middle line:84%
And I propose to deal
with it with these trees,

00:43:52.645 --> 00:43:54.270 align:middle line:84%
you can imagine being
someone who says,

00:43:54.270 --> 00:43:57.540 align:middle line:84%
no, look, "in my
pajamas" is just vague.

00:43:57.540 --> 00:43:59.400 align:middle line:84%
Somebody's got to be
wearing the pajamas,

00:43:59.400 --> 00:44:03.240 align:middle line:84%
and you figure it
out from context

00:44:03.240 --> 00:44:05.220 align:middle line:84%
whether it's me or the
elephant, and that's it.

00:44:05.220 --> 00:44:07.650 align:middle line:90%
There's no need for any trees.

00:44:07.650 --> 00:44:09.570 align:middle line:84%
Just so we're clear,
this is not the right way

00:44:09.570 --> 00:44:11.195 align:middle line:84%
to think about this,
but it's something

00:44:11.195 --> 00:44:13.260 align:middle line:90%
you could imagine thinking.

00:44:13.260 --> 00:44:16.140 align:middle line:84%
Here-- so the stuff we talked
about in the last slide

00:44:16.140 --> 00:44:20.160 align:middle line:84%
is one reason not to think
about things that way.

00:44:20.160 --> 00:44:22.890 align:middle line:84%
Constituency tests
seem to nail down

00:44:22.890 --> 00:44:24.870 align:middle line:84%
where the pajamas
are in the structure,

00:44:24.870 --> 00:44:27.480 align:middle line:84%
and that forces you to
one reading or another.

00:44:27.480 --> 00:44:30.630 align:middle line:84%
Here's another kind of
sentence that also suggests--

00:44:30.630 --> 00:44:33.310 align:middle line:84%
where we also make a prediction,
which I think is correct.

00:44:33.310 --> 00:44:37.200 align:middle line:84%
So if I say "I shot the elephant
in my pajamas in a tuxedo"--

00:44:37.200 --> 00:44:39.750 align:middle line:90%


00:44:39.750 --> 00:44:42.480 align:middle line:90%
I think that's grammatical--

00:44:42.480 --> 00:44:44.970 align:middle line:84%
and I think it's a sentence
on which somebody is wearing

00:44:44.970 --> 00:44:48.360 align:middle line:84%
pajamas and somebody
is wearing a tuxedo,

00:44:48.360 --> 00:44:52.010 align:middle line:90%
who's wearing the pajamas?

00:44:52.010 --> 00:44:53.690 align:middle line:84%
The elephant's
wearing the pajamas

00:44:53.690 --> 00:44:55.895 align:middle line:90%
and I am wearing the tuxedo.

00:44:55.895 --> 00:44:57.395 align:middle line:84%
Does anybody not
have that reading--

00:44:57.395 --> 00:45:01.610 align:middle line:84%
or have instead the reading
where I am wearing the pajamas

00:45:01.610 --> 00:45:04.760 align:middle line:84%
and the elephant is
wearing a tuxedo?

00:45:04.760 --> 00:45:08.810 align:middle line:84%
Please put a cast out
of your mind-- hold on--

00:45:08.810 --> 00:45:10.310 align:middle line:84%
readings in which
the pajamas are

00:45:10.310 --> 00:45:11.780 align:middle line:90%
inside the tuxedo or something.

00:45:11.780 --> 00:45:12.950 align:middle line:90%
[LAUGHTER]

00:45:12.950 --> 00:45:15.320 align:middle line:84%
I can see all of you
thinking of alternatives,

00:45:15.320 --> 00:45:19.010 align:middle line:84%
but just try to imagine
cases where one person-- one

00:45:19.010 --> 00:45:21.440 align:middle line:84%
entity is wearing
pajamas and another one

00:45:21.440 --> 00:45:23.532 align:middle line:90%
is wearing the tuxedo.

00:45:23.532 --> 00:45:25.490 align:middle line:84%
If that's what's happening--
yes, [INAUDIBLE]??

00:45:25.490 --> 00:45:26.407 align:middle line:90%
AUDIENCE: [INAUDIBLE].

00:45:26.407 --> 00:45:28.880 align:middle line:84%
NORVIN RICHARDS: No, the
pajamas are not the tuxedo.

00:45:28.880 --> 00:45:30.575 align:middle line:90%
[LAUGHTER]

00:45:30.575 --> 00:45:32.360 align:middle line:90%
Ah!

00:45:32.360 --> 00:45:35.330 align:middle line:90%
MIT undergrads-- man.

00:45:35.330 --> 00:45:38.912 align:middle line:84%
No, there are two sets of
clothing and two entities,

00:45:38.912 --> 00:45:41.120 align:middle line:84%
and I am also not an elephant
just in case any of you

00:45:41.120 --> 00:45:43.700 align:middle line:90%
were thinking of that.

00:45:43.700 --> 00:45:46.970 align:middle line:84%
And the clothes are
not nested, or mixed,

00:45:46.970 --> 00:45:47.910 align:middle line:90%
or anything like that.

00:45:47.910 --> 00:45:50.030 align:middle line:84%
There is a set of pajamas
and there is a tuxedo,

00:45:50.030 --> 00:45:51.110 align:middle line:84%
and somebody is
wearing the pajamas,

00:45:51.110 --> 00:45:52.190 align:middle line:90%
somebody is wearing the tuxedo.

00:45:52.190 --> 00:45:52.970 align:middle line:90%
Who's in what?

00:45:52.970 --> 00:45:55.100 align:middle line:84%
I just asserted the
elephant is in the pajamas

00:45:55.100 --> 00:45:56.790 align:middle line:90%
and I'm in the tuxedo.

00:45:56.790 --> 00:45:59.920 align:middle line:84%
It can't be the
other way around.

00:45:59.920 --> 00:46:01.270 align:middle line:90%
And we can see why.

00:46:01.270 --> 00:46:02.345 align:middle line:90%
If I was smart enough--

00:46:02.345 --> 00:46:03.970 align:middle line:84%
we'll see if I was
smart enough to draw

00:46:03.970 --> 00:46:05.012 align:middle line:90%
a tree in the next slide.

00:46:05.012 --> 00:46:05.680 align:middle line:90%
Yes, I was!

00:46:05.680 --> 00:46:08.050 align:middle line:90%
Hot dog.

00:46:08.050 --> 00:46:08.728 align:middle line:90%
Why?

00:46:08.728 --> 00:46:11.020 align:middle line:84%
Well, because if you're going
to have two prepositional

00:46:11.020 --> 00:46:13.870 align:middle line:84%
phrases-- "in my pajamas"
and "in a tuxedo"--

00:46:13.870 --> 00:46:16.300 align:middle line:84%
and you're going to
attach them in the ways

00:46:16.300 --> 00:46:18.910 align:middle line:84%
that we've been attaching
prepositional phrases before,

00:46:18.910 --> 00:46:24.000 align:middle line:84%
this is the only way to
do it to get this order.

00:46:24.000 --> 00:46:27.490 align:middle line:84%
Unless you're going to cross
lines or something like that,

00:46:27.490 --> 00:46:29.880 align:middle line:84%
this is the only way to
get this order of words.

00:46:29.880 --> 00:46:33.660 align:middle line:84%
So "in my pajamas" has to modify
"elephant" and "in the tuxedo"

00:46:33.660 --> 00:46:36.120 align:middle line:84%
has to modify the verb phrase,
and that's the only reading

00:46:36.120 --> 00:46:38.840 align:middle line:90%
this, in fact, has.

00:46:38.840 --> 00:46:43.070 align:middle line:84%
So day two of syntax and we're
already successfully predicting

00:46:43.070 --> 00:46:46.070 align:middle line:84%
facts about reasonably
complicated sentences

00:46:46.070 --> 00:46:48.755 align:middle line:84%
involving too much clothing
in inappropriate places.

00:46:48.755 --> 00:46:51.860 align:middle line:90%


00:46:51.860 --> 00:46:52.370 align:middle line:90%
Yeah?

00:46:52.370 --> 00:46:54.537 align:middle line:84%
AUDIENCE: We haven't really
discussed this directly,

00:46:54.537 --> 00:46:57.007 align:middle line:84%
but is it a rule that you
can't cross lines in a tree?

00:46:57.007 --> 00:46:59.340 align:middle line:84%
NORVIN RICHARDS: I hereby
declare that it is a rule that

00:46:59.340 --> 00:47:00.870 align:middle line:90%
you cannot cross lines in trees.

00:47:00.870 --> 00:47:04.183 align:middle line:84%
Yes, you're right, we
have not discussed that.

00:47:04.183 --> 00:47:06.600 align:middle line:84%
Back in the day, I would have
called this the Ghostbusters

00:47:06.600 --> 00:47:08.275 align:middle line:84%
rule just because
the Ghostbusters had

00:47:08.275 --> 00:47:09.900 align:middle line:84%
a rule that you
couldn't cross streams.

00:47:09.900 --> 00:47:10.400 align:middle line:90%
Never mind.

00:47:10.400 --> 00:47:12.930 align:middle line:90%


00:47:12.930 --> 00:47:14.220 align:middle line:90%
Yeah-- other questions?

00:47:14.220 --> 00:47:17.770 align:middle line:90%


00:47:17.770 --> 00:47:20.890 align:middle line:84%
Now, I have this vague
sense that some of you

00:47:20.890 --> 00:47:23.350 align:middle line:84%
would like to see what the rest
of the sentence looks like.

00:47:23.350 --> 00:47:24.058 align:middle line:90%
Call it a hunch--

00:47:24.058 --> 00:47:24.758 align:middle line:90%
I don't know.

00:47:24.758 --> 00:47:26.800 align:middle line:84%
When you've been an
instructor as long as I have,

00:47:26.800 --> 00:47:29.920 align:middle line:84%
you get these vague hints like
the fact that 12 of you have

00:47:29.920 --> 00:47:31.838 align:middle line:84%
asked me where is
the subject going--

00:47:31.838 --> 00:47:33.880 align:middle line:84%
where should I put "will"--
how do we do the rest

00:47:33.880 --> 00:47:36.760 align:middle line:84%
of the sentence-- why, oh, why
are you only showing us verb

00:47:36.760 --> 00:47:38.360 align:middle line:84%
phrases and not
entire sentences?

00:47:38.360 --> 00:47:41.173 align:middle line:84%
So let's do the rest
of the sentence.

00:47:41.173 --> 00:47:43.090 align:middle line:84%
To do the rest of the
sentence-- first of all,

00:47:43.090 --> 00:47:46.180 align:middle line:84%
"the girl" is a noun phrase
that's kind of boring,

00:47:46.180 --> 00:47:48.340 align:middle line:84%
but "girl" is like "the
book" or "the garage."

00:47:48.340 --> 00:47:53.980 align:middle line:84%
And for "will," we are going
to invent a new kind of node.

00:47:53.980 --> 00:47:55.880 align:middle line:84%
We're going to call
it T for tense.

00:47:55.880 --> 00:47:59.540 align:middle line:90%


00:47:59.540 --> 00:48:03.163 align:middle line:84%
Just to prepare you
for disappointment,

00:48:03.163 --> 00:48:04.580 align:middle line:84%
there are all kinds
of things that

00:48:04.580 --> 00:48:08.450 align:middle line:84%
can go under T. You can
also say "The girl can find

00:48:08.450 --> 00:48:11.060 align:middle line:84%
the book in the garage,"
or "The girl might

00:48:11.060 --> 00:48:13.550 align:middle line:90%
find the book in the garage."

00:48:13.550 --> 00:48:15.212 align:middle line:90%
I can't talk today.

00:48:15.212 --> 00:48:16.670 align:middle line:84%
So there are variety
of things that

00:48:16.670 --> 00:48:21.860 align:middle line:84%
can go under T, not all of
which it's plausible to think

00:48:21.860 --> 00:48:23.390 align:middle line:90%
of as instances of tense--

00:48:23.390 --> 00:48:26.630 align:middle line:84%
like "might" is
probably not a tense.

00:48:26.630 --> 00:48:28.718 align:middle line:84%
If you look it up in
traditional grammar,

00:48:28.718 --> 00:48:30.260 align:middle line:84%
they're not going
to call it a tense,

00:48:30.260 --> 00:48:31.430 align:middle line:84%
but we're going to
call it a tense.

00:48:31.430 --> 00:48:33.140 align:middle line:84%
We basically just
need a name for it.

00:48:33.140 --> 00:48:35.870 align:middle line:90%
So there's this thing.

00:48:35.870 --> 00:48:42.380 align:middle line:84%
Notice it's not a verb, so you
can't say things like the girl

00:48:42.380 --> 00:48:45.110 align:middle line:90%
will the book.

00:48:45.110 --> 00:48:47.240 align:middle line:84%
It's something else,
and our name for it

00:48:47.240 --> 00:48:48.290 align:middle line:90%
is going to be "tense."

00:48:48.290 --> 00:48:49.520 align:middle line:84%
People have called
it other things.

00:48:49.520 --> 00:48:51.353 align:middle line:84%
They've called it the
auxiliary or whatever,

00:48:51.353 --> 00:48:54.150 align:middle line:84%
but tense is a conventional
name for it in the literature,

00:48:54.150 --> 00:48:55.760 align:middle line:90%
so I'm going to call it that.

00:48:55.760 --> 00:48:56.630 align:middle line:90%
Yeah?

00:48:56.630 --> 00:48:59.870 align:middle line:84%
AUDIENCE: So how would you
differentiate the tense

00:48:59.870 --> 00:49:01.938 align:middle line:90%
from [INAUDIBLE]?

00:49:01.938 --> 00:49:03.355 align:middle line:84%
NORVIN RICHARDS:
Well-- so those--

00:49:03.355 --> 00:49:04.855 align:middle line:84%
AUDIENCE: "The girl
quickly found--"

00:49:04.855 --> 00:49:07.230 align:middle line:84%
NORVIN RICHARDS: "Quickly
found the book in the garage"--

00:49:07.230 --> 00:49:08.320 align:middle line:90%
yeah, good question.

00:49:08.320 --> 00:49:10.620 align:middle line:90%
Let me give you the--

00:49:10.620 --> 00:49:17.040 align:middle line:84%
let me give you an answer that
I won't be able to explain soon,

00:49:17.040 --> 00:49:19.740 align:middle line:90%
but that's a very nice question.

00:49:19.740 --> 00:49:21.460 align:middle line:90%
So the girl will find it--

00:49:21.460 --> 00:49:22.710 align:middle line:90%
I'll just say that--

00:49:22.710 --> 00:49:31.080 align:middle line:90%
and the girl quickly found it.

00:49:31.080 --> 00:49:33.600 align:middle line:84%
And you want to know what's the
difference between these two

00:49:33.600 --> 00:49:35.063 align:middle line:90%
things.

00:49:35.063 --> 00:49:35.980 align:middle line:90%
Here's one difference.

00:49:35.980 --> 00:49:40.290 align:middle line:84%
There are a bunch of phenomena
that this gets to participate

00:49:40.290 --> 00:49:41.370 align:middle line:90%
in this doesn't.

00:49:41.370 --> 00:49:45.720 align:middle line:84%
So for example, if I want to
make this into a question,

00:49:45.720 --> 00:49:52.643 align:middle line:84%
I can ask the question,
"Will the girl find it?"

00:49:52.643 --> 00:49:54.810 align:middle line:84%
where I put "will" at the
beginning of the sentence,

00:49:54.810 --> 00:49:57.692 align:middle line:84%
and that makes the
sentence a question.

00:49:57.692 --> 00:50:00.150 align:middle line:84%
You can put "quickly" at the
beginning of the sentence too,

00:50:00.150 --> 00:50:01.320 align:middle line:90%
I guess--

00:50:01.320 --> 00:50:05.160 align:middle line:90%
"Quickly, the girl found it."

00:50:05.160 --> 00:50:08.870 align:middle line:84%
That's a grammatical sentence,
but it's not a question.

00:50:08.870 --> 00:50:11.190 align:middle line:90%
It doesn't make it a question.

00:50:11.190 --> 00:50:14.970 align:middle line:84%
So that gives us some
reason to want these

00:50:14.970 --> 00:50:17.430 align:middle line:90%
to have different statuses.

00:50:17.430 --> 00:50:19.350 align:middle line:84%
I'll give you another
reason to have

00:50:19.350 --> 00:50:22.140 align:middle line:90%
them have different statuses.

00:50:22.140 --> 00:50:24.860 align:middle line:90%
If I ask you--

00:50:24.860 --> 00:50:27.050 align:middle line:90%
well, is this true?

00:50:27.050 --> 00:50:28.010 align:middle line:90%
Maybe I'm about to lie.

00:50:28.010 --> 00:50:29.900 align:middle line:84%
Maybe I should quit
while I'm ahead.

00:50:29.900 --> 00:50:30.800 align:middle line:90%
I think this is true.

00:50:30.800 --> 00:50:33.290 align:middle line:84%
If I ask you, "Who's
going to find the book?"

00:50:33.290 --> 00:50:36.500 align:middle line:90%
you can answer "The girl will."

00:50:36.500 --> 00:50:38.970 align:middle line:84%
But if I ask you
"Who found the book?"

00:50:38.970 --> 00:50:41.830 align:middle line:84%
it would be odd for you
to say "The girl quickly."

00:50:41.830 --> 00:50:43.730 align:middle line:90%
That's-- I think-- is that true?

00:50:43.730 --> 00:50:45.640 align:middle line:90%
Did I just lie?

00:50:45.640 --> 00:50:46.750 align:middle line:90%
I think that's true.

00:50:46.750 --> 00:50:48.250 align:middle line:84%
So that's another
reason to think

00:50:48.250 --> 00:50:49.810 align:middle line:84%
there's some difference
between "will" and "quickly."

00:50:49.810 --> 00:50:51.370 align:middle line:90%
Now, what's the difference?

00:50:51.370 --> 00:50:53.548 align:middle line:84%
Well, we'll get to that,
but-- or like how long

00:50:53.548 --> 00:50:55.090 align:middle line:84%
are we going to
explain these things,

00:50:55.090 --> 00:50:56.332 align:middle line:90%
and we'll get to that too.

00:50:56.332 --> 00:50:58.540 align:middle line:84%
This is just meant to convince
you that maybe they're

00:50:58.540 --> 00:50:59.510 align:middle line:90%
different things--

00:50:59.510 --> 00:51:02.270 align:middle line:90%
they have different properties.

00:51:02.270 --> 00:51:04.130 align:middle line:90%
OK, cool.

00:51:04.130 --> 00:51:05.330 align:middle line:90%
So far, so good.

00:51:05.330 --> 00:51:07.790 align:middle line:84%
Now, we need to do
some more merging.

00:51:07.790 --> 00:51:09.650 align:middle line:90%
Here's what we'll do.

00:51:09.650 --> 00:51:12.140 align:middle line:84%
We'll merge T with
the verb phrase,

00:51:12.140 --> 00:51:15.510 align:middle line:84%
and then we'll merge "the
girl" with the result

00:51:15.510 --> 00:51:18.440 align:middle line:84%
so that the entire
sentence will be a TP--

00:51:18.440 --> 00:51:20.720 align:middle line:90%
a tense phrase.

00:51:20.720 --> 00:51:22.070 align:middle line:90%
Everything is a phrase.

00:51:22.070 --> 00:51:24.027 align:middle line:84%
There are these, there
are noun phrases,

00:51:24.027 --> 00:51:26.360 align:middle line:84%
there are prepositional
phrases, there are verb phrases.

00:51:26.360 --> 00:51:28.693 align:middle line:84%
And now, we have a new kind
of phrase-- a tense phrase--

00:51:28.693 --> 00:51:32.450 align:middle line:84%
which is the result
of merging tense will

00:51:32.450 --> 00:51:34.640 align:middle line:90%
with a bunch of things.

00:51:34.640 --> 00:51:39.560 align:middle line:84%
There-- that's how
we'll do sentences Cool.

00:51:39.560 --> 00:51:41.655 align:middle line:90%
That was easy and painless.

00:51:41.655 --> 00:51:42.155 align:middle line:90%
Yeah?

00:51:42.155 --> 00:51:44.840 align:middle line:84%
AUDIENCE: What does
topicalize mean again exactly?

00:51:44.840 --> 00:51:47.420 align:middle line:84%
NORVIN RICHARDS:
Oh, it's the name

00:51:47.420 --> 00:51:51.050 align:middle line:84%
that we've got for taking
constituents, putting them

00:51:51.050 --> 00:51:53.720 align:middle line:84%
at the beginning
of the sentence,

00:51:53.720 --> 00:51:58.310 align:middle line:84%
and it adds some kind
of oomph to the thing

00:51:58.310 --> 00:52:00.950 align:middle line:84%
that you put at the
beginning of the sentence.

00:52:00.950 --> 00:52:02.870 align:middle line:84%
So if I say things
like "in the garage,

00:52:02.870 --> 00:52:06.800 align:middle line:84%
the girl will find the book,"
that's a sentence of English.

00:52:06.800 --> 00:52:09.020 align:middle line:84%
It's not the most
natural way to say it--

00:52:09.020 --> 00:52:11.270 align:middle line:84%
you would only say
it if you wanted

00:52:11.270 --> 00:52:14.540 align:middle line:84%
to put some kind of special
emphasis on the garage.

00:52:14.540 --> 00:52:18.060 align:middle line:84%
And we will not
ever, in this class,

00:52:18.060 --> 00:52:19.520 align:middle line:84%
try to be more
definite than that

00:52:19.520 --> 00:52:21.020 align:middle line:84%
about the meaning
of topicalization.

00:52:21.020 --> 00:52:25.790 align:middle line:84%
But it's a handy phenomenon
for finding constituents.

00:52:25.790 --> 00:52:27.190 align:middle line:90%
Yeah?

00:52:27.190 --> 00:52:31.510 align:middle line:84%
AUDIENCE: Every node on
the tree is a constituent?

00:52:31.510 --> 00:52:33.850 align:middle line:90%
NORVIN RICHARDS: Yes.

00:52:33.850 --> 00:52:36.970 align:middle line:84%
AUDIENCE: What is "will find
the book in the garage"?

00:52:36.970 --> 00:52:38.710 align:middle line:90%
NORVIN RICHARDS: It's a T bar.

00:52:38.710 --> 00:52:41.620 align:middle line:84%
AUDIENCE: Well, yes, but
we have constituency tests

00:52:41.620 --> 00:52:44.890 align:middle line:84%
that we use to tell whether
something is a constituent.

00:52:44.890 --> 00:52:48.522 align:middle line:84%
Does that work for
a tense phrase?

00:52:48.522 --> 00:52:49.480 align:middle line:90%
NORVIN RICHARDS: Yeah--

00:52:49.480 --> 00:52:50.605 align:middle line:90%
OK, that's a good question.

00:52:50.605 --> 00:52:52.462 align:middle line:90%
[LAUGHTER]

00:52:52.462 --> 00:52:53.420 align:middle line:90%
That's a good question.

00:52:53.420 --> 00:52:53.920 align:middle line:90%
Let's see.

00:52:53.920 --> 00:52:56.860 align:middle line:90%
So let me-- so let's see.

00:52:56.860 --> 00:52:58.580 align:middle line:90%
You would like to know--

00:52:58.580 --> 00:52:59.830 align:middle line:90%
is this what you're asking me?

00:52:59.830 --> 00:53:02.320 align:middle line:90%
Let's see.

00:53:02.320 --> 00:53:03.940 align:middle line:90%
We've got three things--

00:53:03.940 --> 00:53:06.880 align:middle line:90%


00:53:06.880 --> 00:53:14.900 align:middle line:84%
the noun phrase, "the
girl," and the T "will,"

00:53:14.900 --> 00:53:18.560 align:middle line:84%
and the verb phrase find
"the book in the garage."

00:53:18.560 --> 00:53:19.120 align:middle line:90%
Oh, here.

00:53:19.120 --> 00:53:27.280 align:middle line:90%


00:53:27.280 --> 00:53:29.720 align:middle line:84%
We have a technical
term for this.

00:53:29.720 --> 00:53:32.830 align:middle line:90%
It's called a triangle.

00:53:32.830 --> 00:53:35.260 align:middle line:84%
It's used when you
do not wish to draw

00:53:35.260 --> 00:53:37.270 align:middle line:90%
the inside of a structure.

00:53:37.270 --> 00:53:40.270 align:middle line:84%
So this is just an
abbreviation for that.

00:53:40.270 --> 00:53:41.230 align:middle line:90%
Is that OK?

00:53:41.230 --> 00:53:42.730 align:middle line:84%
So I've just used
a triangle here so

00:53:42.730 --> 00:53:44.605 align:middle line:84%
that I won't have to
draw that entire phrase.

00:53:44.605 --> 00:53:46.990 align:middle line:84%
What I really need is
like a stamp or something

00:53:46.990 --> 00:53:50.230 align:middle line:84%
that I can use on the blackboard
that will create these phrase

00:53:50.230 --> 00:53:52.120 align:middle line:90%
structures for me.

00:53:52.120 --> 00:53:54.160 align:middle line:84%
We'll tell you on
problem sets if we

00:53:54.160 --> 00:53:55.525 align:middle line:90%
want you to not use triangles.

00:53:55.525 --> 00:53:57.400 align:middle line:84%
Sometimes, we'll ask
you not to use triangles

00:53:57.400 --> 00:53:59.590 align:middle line:84%
so that we can make
you show us everything

00:53:59.590 --> 00:54:01.760 align:middle line:84%
that you think about the
insides of structures,

00:54:01.760 --> 00:54:03.920 align:middle line:84%
but for now, I'll
use a triangle.

00:54:03.920 --> 00:54:05.452 align:middle line:84%
So I just casually
said, hey, I've

00:54:05.452 --> 00:54:07.660 align:middle line:84%
got a good idea-- we'll
merge "will" first with this,

00:54:07.660 --> 00:54:10.623 align:middle line:84%
and then we'll merge
the result with that.

00:54:10.623 --> 00:54:12.790 align:middle line:84%
If we're doing merge the
way we've been doing merge,

00:54:12.790 --> 00:54:14.740 align:middle line:84%
there's only one other
option, which is this.

00:54:14.740 --> 00:54:20.770 align:middle line:90%


00:54:20.770 --> 00:54:24.580 align:middle line:84%
And so you would like
to know, how do we know

00:54:24.580 --> 00:54:28.180 align:middle line:90%
that it's what's on the slide?

00:54:28.180 --> 00:54:29.590 align:middle line:84%
How do we know
that it's not what

00:54:29.590 --> 00:54:31.570 align:middle line:90%
I've got here on the board?

00:54:31.570 --> 00:54:39.670 align:middle line:84%
Just so nobody gets confused,
this is not the right tree.

00:54:39.670 --> 00:54:41.500 align:middle line:84%
That's the right
tree, but now let

00:54:41.500 --> 00:54:44.140 align:middle line:84%
me see if I can come up with
anything that will convince you

00:54:44.140 --> 00:54:46.480 align:middle line:90%
that it's the right tree.

00:54:46.480 --> 00:54:53.840 align:middle line:84%
First of all, if we tried to
use topicalization, which is

00:54:53.840 --> 00:54:57.300 align:middle line:90%
the test that we had before--

00:54:57.300 --> 00:54:59.750 align:middle line:84%
if we tried to use it on
the tree here and we tried

00:54:59.750 --> 00:55:03.020 align:middle line:84%
to topicalize T-bar,
we'd end up with--

00:55:03.020 --> 00:55:05.360 align:middle line:84%
"I said the girl would
find the book in the garage

00:55:05.360 --> 00:55:10.200 align:middle line:84%
and will find the book
in the garage, the girl."

00:55:10.200 --> 00:55:12.960 align:middle line:84%
Now, as I mentioned
before, my grasp of English

00:55:12.960 --> 00:55:16.210 align:middle line:84%
is now a little shaky
after years of abuse,

00:55:16.210 --> 00:55:18.840 align:middle line:90%
but I think that's not great.

00:55:18.840 --> 00:55:21.180 align:middle line:90%
Do people agree?

00:55:21.180 --> 00:55:24.030 align:middle line:84%
So if there is a
constituent T-bar here--

00:55:24.030 --> 00:55:26.998 align:middle line:84%
I tried to soften you up for
this possibility earlier--

00:55:26.998 --> 00:55:29.040 align:middle line:84%
we need it to be the kind
of constituent to which

00:55:29.040 --> 00:55:30.510 align:middle line:90%
topicalization can't apply.

00:55:30.510 --> 00:55:33.430 align:middle line:84%
That is, topicalization doesn't
get to apply to everything.

00:55:33.430 --> 00:55:35.670 align:middle line:84%
And I think I also
floated the possibility

00:55:35.670 --> 00:55:37.290 align:middle line:84%
that it cares about
whether you're

00:55:37.290 --> 00:55:40.260 align:middle line:90%
looking at a P or a bar--

00:55:40.260 --> 00:55:44.580 align:middle line:84%
that you can't topicalize bars,
you can only topicalize P's.

00:55:44.580 --> 00:55:46.260 align:middle line:84%
This might be
another case where we

00:55:46.260 --> 00:55:48.660 align:middle line:84%
want to take that
possibility seriously.

00:55:48.660 --> 00:55:50.370 align:middle line:90%
Yeah?

00:55:50.370 --> 00:55:55.340 align:middle line:84%
AUDIENCE: It seems like
that-- the one on the board

00:55:55.340 --> 00:55:57.090 align:middle line:84%
works better as a
constituent, because you

00:55:57.090 --> 00:56:00.090 align:middle line:84%
could be like, "Who will
find the book in the garage?"

00:56:00.090 --> 00:56:02.430 align:middle line:90%
and then say "The girl will."

00:56:02.430 --> 00:56:05.382 align:middle line:84%
But if you say "What will
the girl do," you can't say

00:56:05.382 --> 00:56:06.840 align:middle line:84%
"Will find the book
in the garage."

00:56:06.840 --> 00:56:08.340 align:middle line:84%
NORVIN RICHARDS:
You can't say "will

00:56:08.340 --> 00:56:09.840 align:middle line:90%
find the book in the garage"--

00:56:09.840 --> 00:56:15.120 align:middle line:84%
if-- I am astonished by
what you have just said.

00:56:15.120 --> 00:56:17.190 align:middle line:84%
If I've just-- you've
just said "The girl will

00:56:17.190 --> 00:56:18.480 align:middle line:90%
find the book in the garage."

00:56:18.480 --> 00:56:23.230 align:middle line:84%
Can I say "Will find
the book in the garage?"

00:56:23.230 --> 00:56:25.990 align:middle line:90%
Some think yes, others think no.

00:56:25.990 --> 00:56:31.190 align:middle line:84%
We'll have a wrestling
match outside later.

00:56:31.190 --> 00:56:32.752 align:middle line:90%
AUDIENCE: That makes sense.

00:56:32.752 --> 00:56:34.210 align:middle line:84%
But if you're
astonished, you could

00:56:34.210 --> 00:56:36.252 align:middle line:84%
say "The girl will find
the book in the garage"--

00:56:36.252 --> 00:56:37.310 align:middle line:90%
"The girl will?"

00:56:37.310 --> 00:56:39.435 align:middle line:84%
NORVIN RICHARDS: (QUESTIONINGLY)
"The girl will?"--

00:56:39.435 --> 00:56:42.730 align:middle line:90%
ah, yeah, that's true.

00:56:42.730 --> 00:56:46.945 align:middle line:84%
You guys are discovering a
phenomenon called ellipsis.

00:56:46.945 --> 00:56:50.110 align:middle line:90%


00:56:50.110 --> 00:56:53.800 align:middle line:84%
Ellipsis is a
phenomenon whereby you

00:56:53.800 --> 00:56:57.430 align:middle line:84%
can take chunks of the sentence
and fail to pronounce them.

00:56:57.430 --> 00:57:01.910 align:middle line:84%
You can remove chunks
of the sentence.

00:57:01.910 --> 00:57:04.480 align:middle line:84%
So if I ask you a
question like, "Who will

00:57:04.480 --> 00:57:06.280 align:middle line:90%
find the book in the garage?"

00:57:06.280 --> 00:57:08.860 align:middle line:90%
you can say "The girl will."

00:57:08.860 --> 00:57:10.840 align:middle line:84%
Or I can say things
like "The boys will

00:57:10.840 --> 00:57:13.225 align:middle line:84%
find the book in the garage
and the girl will too."

00:57:13.225 --> 00:57:16.090 align:middle line:90%


00:57:16.090 --> 00:57:20.105 align:middle line:84%
All of these-- the way
we would talk about them

00:57:20.105 --> 00:57:22.480 align:middle line:84%
in terms of tree, like the
one that's on the slide there,

00:57:22.480 --> 00:57:24.340 align:middle line:84%
is in terms of what's
called VP ellipses.

00:57:24.340 --> 00:57:24.840 align:middle line:90%
It.

00:57:24.840 --> 00:57:28.870 align:middle line:84%
Is this process of
silencing constituents--

00:57:28.870 --> 00:57:31.150 align:middle line:90%
silencing phrases--

00:57:31.150 --> 00:57:33.520 align:middle line:84%
has applied to the
verb phrase when

00:57:33.520 --> 00:57:43.810 align:middle line:84%
you say things like "Who will
find the book, (blah, blah,

00:57:43.810 --> 00:57:45.070 align:middle line:90%
blah)?"--

00:57:45.070 --> 00:57:45.790 align:middle line:90%
"The girl will."

00:57:45.790 --> 00:57:48.590 align:middle line:90%


00:57:48.590 --> 00:57:50.290 align:middle line:84%
So there's a blank
here, and the blank

00:57:50.290 --> 00:57:56.080 align:middle line:84%
is understood as being the same
as the preceding verb phrase.

00:57:56.080 --> 00:58:00.098 align:middle line:84%
And VP ellipsis is
a huge phenomenon,

00:58:00.098 --> 00:58:02.140 align:middle line:84%
which we will get a chance
to talk about actually

00:58:02.140 --> 00:58:06.070 align:middle line:84%
because it's going to be
useful in a couple of weeks

00:58:06.070 --> 00:58:07.630 align:middle line:90%
for other phenomena.

00:58:07.630 --> 00:58:10.330 align:middle line:90%


00:58:10.330 --> 00:58:13.300 align:middle line:84%
Notice-- I'm trying
to think if there's

00:58:13.300 --> 00:58:15.850 align:middle line:90%
a way that I can convince you.

00:58:15.850 --> 00:58:18.250 align:middle line:84%
And so that's not where
you were going with this.

00:58:18.250 --> 00:58:20.980 align:middle line:84%
Where you were going with it
was, hey, look, "The girl will"

00:58:20.980 --> 00:58:21.748 align:middle line:90%
is a constituent.

00:58:21.748 --> 00:58:23.290 align:middle line:84%
There is a thing
that we want to have

00:58:23.290 --> 00:58:26.770 align:middle line:84%
be a constituent-- we want
it to be a T-bar like here,

00:58:26.770 --> 00:58:29.230 align:middle line:84%
whereas what I'm
saying is, no, we

00:58:29.230 --> 00:58:31.492 align:middle line:84%
want the structure on
the slide, and there

00:58:31.492 --> 00:58:32.950 align:middle line:84%
is such a thing as
VP ellipsis that

00:58:32.950 --> 00:58:37.930 align:middle line:84%
allows you to fail to
pronounce verb phrases.

00:58:37.930 --> 00:58:43.610 align:middle line:84%
Let me see if I can come
up with another test that

00:58:43.610 --> 00:58:48.985 align:middle line:84%
will help you believe in
one of these over there.

00:58:48.985 --> 00:58:51.560 align:middle line:84%
There are other
phenomena that people

00:58:51.560 --> 00:58:54.402 align:middle line:84%
use to diagnose
constituent structure,

00:58:54.402 --> 00:58:56.360 align:middle line:84%
and I'm trying to think
if there are any that I

00:58:56.360 --> 00:59:00.200 align:middle line:90%
can introduce painlessly.

00:59:00.200 --> 00:59:02.240 align:middle line:90%
You-- ah, no.

00:59:02.240 --> 00:59:03.987 align:middle line:84%
So I was about to
introduce coordination,

00:59:03.987 --> 00:59:04.820 align:middle line:90%
but I think I won't.

00:59:04.820 --> 00:59:05.403 align:middle line:90%
You know what?

00:59:05.403 --> 00:59:07.610 align:middle line:84%
Your TAs will prove
to you that it's

00:59:07.610 --> 00:59:09.830 align:middle line:84%
the tree on the left and
not the tree on the right

00:59:09.830 --> 00:59:13.900 align:middle line:90%
in the recitation section.

00:59:13.900 --> 00:59:15.650 align:middle line:84%
So definitely go to
the recitation session

00:59:15.650 --> 00:59:18.800 align:middle line:84%
this week, because your TAs
will help you with this.

00:59:18.800 --> 00:59:23.180 align:middle line:84%
I don't want to go too far down
this path because there are--

00:59:23.180 --> 00:59:27.710 align:middle line:84%
if I try to show you the
constituent tests that

00:59:27.710 --> 00:59:30.570 align:middle line:84%
would help us to find that,
I think we'll be in trouble.

00:59:30.570 --> 00:59:35.740 align:middle line:84%
There is one-- well,
maybe I can do this now.

00:59:35.740 --> 00:59:36.820 align:middle line:90%
Can I do this now?

00:59:36.820 --> 00:59:40.970 align:middle line:90%


00:59:40.970 --> 00:59:42.950 align:middle line:84%
I'm going to get
in so much trouble.

00:59:42.950 --> 00:59:45.137 align:middle line:90%
This is not going to go well.

00:59:45.137 --> 00:59:47.720 align:middle line:84%
I'm going to regret doing this,
and we're almost out of clock.

00:59:47.720 --> 00:59:49.987 align:middle line:84%
Tell you what-- I
will prove to you--

00:59:49.987 --> 00:59:52.070 align:middle line:84%
you don't have to-- so you
should go to recitation

00:59:52.070 --> 00:59:54.380 align:middle line:84%
session, but I will prove
to you in the next class

00:59:54.380 --> 00:59:57.140 align:middle line:84%
that that's the right tree
and this is the wrong tree,

00:59:57.140 --> 00:59:59.940 align:middle line:84%
but it's going to
require some setup.

00:59:59.940 --> 01:00:01.730 align:middle line:84%
And if I try to
do the setup now,

01:00:01.730 --> 01:00:04.370 align:middle line:84%
we'll be in the middle of the
setup at the end of class,

01:00:04.370 --> 01:00:06.080 align:middle line:90%
and we'll all go away unhappy.

01:00:06.080 --> 01:00:09.180 align:middle line:84%
So this is a really
excellent question,

01:00:09.180 --> 01:00:13.790 align:middle line:84%
and you're right to be
skeptical and alarmed.

01:00:13.790 --> 01:00:19.160 align:middle line:84%
But everybody write the tree
that's on the board here,

01:00:19.160 --> 01:00:22.280 align:middle line:84%
and I will also reproduce
it on the next set

01:00:22.280 --> 01:00:24.950 align:middle line:90%
of slides for Thursday.

01:00:24.950 --> 01:00:27.260 align:middle line:84%
And then you will go into
spring break understanding

01:00:27.260 --> 01:00:29.390 align:middle line:84%
why I think it's that
tree and not this tree.

01:00:29.390 --> 01:00:33.080 align:middle line:84%
But if I tried to show it to you
now, I'll just hurt all of us.

01:00:33.080 --> 01:00:34.663 align:middle line:90%
Yeah good questions.

01:00:34.663 --> 01:00:36.205 align:middle line:84%
Are there other
questions about this.

01:00:36.205 --> 01:00:38.038 align:middle line:84%
I'll very quickly move
away from this issue.

01:00:38.038 --> 01:00:40.760 align:middle line:90%


01:00:40.760 --> 01:00:45.020 align:middle line:84%
So I want to go back to the
verb phrase just as an exercise

01:00:45.020 --> 01:00:48.872 align:middle line:84%
in making sure that we
all know what we're doing.

01:00:48.872 --> 01:00:50.330 align:middle line:84%
So we're going to
do "I will tickle

01:00:50.330 --> 01:00:52.100 align:middle line:90%
the child with the feather.

01:00:52.100 --> 01:00:55.023 align:middle line:84%
Ambiguous sentence--
does everybody agree?

01:00:55.023 --> 01:00:56.690 align:middle line:84%
So what are the things
that it can mean?

01:00:56.690 --> 01:01:00.550 align:middle line:90%


01:01:00.550 --> 01:01:02.050 align:middle line:84%
Can somebody
paraphrase them for me?

01:01:02.050 --> 01:01:03.300 align:middle line:90%
Joseph?

01:01:03.300 --> 01:01:06.520 align:middle line:84%
AUDIENCE: Either there's a
child that is holding a feather

01:01:06.520 --> 01:01:09.080 align:middle line:84%
and your going to go
tickle him or her,

01:01:09.080 --> 01:01:12.755 align:middle line:84%
or you're going to
go tickle the child,

01:01:12.755 --> 01:01:14.255 align:middle line:84%
and you have a
feather in your hand,

01:01:14.255 --> 01:01:16.050 align:middle line:84%
and you're going
to use the feather.

01:01:16.050 --> 01:01:18.180 align:middle line:84%
NORVIN RICHARDS: Yep,
nicely paraphrased.

01:01:18.180 --> 01:01:21.120 align:middle line:84%
So give me neither
of those things.

01:01:21.120 --> 01:01:27.690 align:middle line:84%
We think that we can build this
in a couple of different ways.

01:01:27.690 --> 01:01:29.430 align:middle line:90%
So "I will tickle the child"--

01:01:29.430 --> 01:01:33.930 align:middle line:84%
we have two places to
attach "with the feather."

01:01:33.930 --> 01:01:35.642 align:middle line:90%
So two trees for this.

01:01:35.642 --> 01:01:37.350 align:middle line:84%
I'm going through this
fast because we're

01:01:37.350 --> 01:01:39.473 align:middle line:84%
running low on time,
but is this all clear?

01:01:39.473 --> 01:01:40.890 align:middle line:84%
This is just the
same kind of tree

01:01:40.890 --> 01:01:44.220 align:middle line:84%
that we've been
drawing up until now.

01:01:44.220 --> 01:01:48.300 align:middle line:84%
And constituency tests,
like topicalization,

01:01:48.300 --> 01:01:51.720 align:middle line:84%
tell us that we're
right to associate

01:01:51.720 --> 01:01:54.130 align:middle line:84%
these two different trees
with two different meanings.

01:01:54.130 --> 01:01:56.965 align:middle line:84%
So these trees are basically
just like the trees we did--

01:01:56.965 --> 01:01:58.590 align:middle line:84%
in fact, they're
identical to the trees

01:01:58.590 --> 01:02:03.120 align:middle line:84%
that we did for "I shot an
elephant in my pajamas."

01:02:03.120 --> 01:02:06.810 align:middle line:84%
The prepositional phrase that's
at the end of the sentence

01:02:06.810 --> 01:02:08.640 align:middle line:84%
can either be part
of the noun phrase

01:02:08.640 --> 01:02:10.223 align:middle line:84%
that's also at the
end of the sentence

01:02:10.223 --> 01:02:12.570 align:middle line:84%
or it can modify
the verb phrase.

01:02:12.570 --> 01:02:15.495 align:middle line:84%
It depends on whether
we're doing, in this case,

01:02:15.495 --> 01:02:17.370 align:middle line:84%
the tickling with the
feather or whether it's

01:02:17.370 --> 01:02:18.828 align:middle line:84%
the child who has
a feather and I'm

01:02:18.828 --> 01:02:21.810 align:middle line:84%
going to tickle them some other
way-- maybe with my fingers.

01:02:21.810 --> 01:02:23.310 align:middle line:84%
And then if we do
topicalization,

01:02:23.310 --> 01:02:25.110 align:middle line:84%
that makes the
meanings collapse.

01:02:25.110 --> 01:02:31.140 align:middle line:84%
So "The child I will tickle
with the feather" only

01:02:31.140 --> 01:02:32.490 align:middle line:90%
has the tree on the left.

01:02:32.490 --> 01:02:34.240 align:middle line:84%
"The child with the
feather I will tickle"

01:02:34.240 --> 01:02:35.990 align:middle line:84%
only has the tree on
the right and it only

01:02:35.990 --> 01:02:37.270 align:middle line:90%
has the meaning on the right.

01:02:37.270 --> 01:02:38.970 align:middle line:90%
Does everybody see that?

01:02:38.970 --> 01:02:40.740 align:middle line:84%
This is review of
where we are so

01:02:40.740 --> 01:02:43.500 align:middle line:84%
far with the
constituent structures--

01:02:43.500 --> 01:02:45.400 align:middle line:90%
constituency tests that we have.

01:02:45.400 --> 01:02:47.970 align:middle line:84%
And as I said, I will
reveal another one to you

01:02:47.970 --> 01:02:50.580 align:middle line:84%
on Thursday, which will help
me to get rid of this tree.

01:02:50.580 --> 01:02:51.360 align:middle line:90%
Yeah?

01:02:51.360 --> 01:02:52.440 align:middle line:84%
AUDIENCE: Oh, so that
second sentence--

01:02:52.440 --> 01:02:54.750 align:middle line:84%
I think this is a feature
of English grammar, which

01:02:54.750 --> 01:02:57.545 align:middle line:84%
is that you could understand
"the child with the feather"

01:02:57.545 --> 01:03:00.720 align:middle line:84%
[INAUDIBLE] as a
nondestructive modifier.

01:03:00.720 --> 01:03:03.270 align:middle line:84%
So that I have a child with
a feather and I will tickle--

01:03:03.270 --> 01:03:05.312 align:middle line:84%
NORVIN RICHARDS: "I will
tickle people generally.

01:03:05.312 --> 01:03:08.880 align:middle line:84%
The child with the
feather, I will tickle."

01:03:08.880 --> 01:03:10.230 align:middle line:90%
Really?

01:03:10.230 --> 01:03:12.270 align:middle line:90%
Maybe.

01:03:12.270 --> 01:03:13.035 align:middle line:90%
If so--

01:03:13.035 --> 01:03:14.220 align:middle line:84%
AUDIENCE: "I, the child
with the feather"--

01:03:14.220 --> 01:03:16.050 align:middle line:84%
NORVIN RICHARDS: "I, the child
with the feather, will tickle."

01:03:16.050 --> 01:03:16.680 align:middle line:90%
AUDIENCE: --[INAUDIBLE].

01:03:16.680 --> 01:03:19.180 align:middle line:84%
NORVIN RICHARDS: I think I want
"the child with the feather"

01:03:19.180 --> 01:03:20.760 align:middle line:90%
to be after "I" for that.

01:03:20.760 --> 01:03:22.410 align:middle line:84%
I think that was
Kateryna's point.

01:03:22.410 --> 01:03:23.160 align:middle line:90%
AUDIENCE: I am the child.

01:03:23.160 --> 01:03:25.243 align:middle line:84%
NORVIN RICHARDS: "I, the
child with the feather"--

01:03:25.243 --> 01:03:30.150 align:middle line:84%
"I, the professor in this
class, demand cookies."

01:03:30.150 --> 01:03:34.450 align:middle line:84%
I think that's grammatical--
even plausible.

01:03:34.450 --> 01:03:34.950 align:middle line:90%
Cool.

01:03:34.950 --> 01:03:36.750 align:middle line:90%
So yeah, good point.

01:03:36.750 --> 01:03:40.975 align:middle line:84%
There could be other
structures to think about here.

01:03:40.975 --> 01:03:42.350 align:middle line:84%
Because we're
almost out of time,

01:03:42.350 --> 01:03:45.500 align:middle line:84%
let me talk quickly
through some terminology,

01:03:45.500 --> 01:03:47.570 align:middle line:90%
and then I'll let you guys go.

01:03:47.570 --> 01:03:49.770 align:middle line:90%
First bit of terminology--

01:03:49.770 --> 01:03:52.280 align:middle line:84%
when people are
talking about trees

01:03:52.280 --> 01:03:55.310 align:middle line:84%
they often use feminine
kinship terminology

01:03:55.310 --> 01:03:59.310 align:middle line:84%
to talk about relationships
between nodes in the tree.

01:03:59.310 --> 01:04:06.740 align:middle line:84%
So if I want to say something
like "I will tickle the child,"

01:04:06.740 --> 01:04:11.330 align:middle line:84%
we have pairs of nodes
that were merged together

01:04:11.330 --> 01:04:12.890 align:middle line:90%
to form larger objects.

01:04:12.890 --> 01:04:15.890 align:middle line:84%
Like in this tree,
we merged the T--

01:04:15.890 --> 01:04:19.160 align:middle line:84%
"will"-- with the verb
phrase-- "tickle the child"--

01:04:19.160 --> 01:04:23.240 align:middle line:84%
to form the T-bar
that's just above them.

01:04:23.240 --> 01:04:25.820 align:middle line:84%
What we say about those
is that they are sisters.

01:04:25.820 --> 01:04:28.310 align:middle line:84%
So if you have two things
that are the two things that

01:04:28.310 --> 01:04:30.500 align:middle line:84%
were merged together to
create one larger thing,

01:04:30.500 --> 01:04:32.870 align:middle line:90%
those two things are sisters.

01:04:32.870 --> 01:04:38.000 align:middle line:84%
Similarly, we say that VP
is the mother of V and NP.

01:04:38.000 --> 01:04:40.970 align:middle line:84%
And we also use the
word "daughter"--

01:04:40.970 --> 01:04:42.577 align:middle line:90%
sorry.

01:04:42.577 --> 01:04:43.910 align:middle line:90%
We also use the word "daughter."

01:04:43.910 --> 01:04:46.202 align:middle line:84%
Although I don't have it on
the slide, I should add it.

01:04:46.202 --> 01:04:48.800 align:middle line:84%
So you say that the V
is the daughter of VP.

01:04:48.800 --> 01:04:50.930 align:middle line:84%
That's as far as people
go with kinship terms,

01:04:50.930 --> 01:04:53.067 align:middle line:84%
so you don't hear
about grandmothers,

01:04:53.067 --> 01:04:54.900 align:middle line:84%
or aunts, or cousins,
or anything like that.

01:04:54.900 --> 01:04:58.130 align:middle line:84%
But people use those terms
to talk about relationships

01:04:58.130 --> 01:05:00.440 align:middle line:90%
between nodes in the tree.

01:05:00.440 --> 01:05:04.460 align:middle line:84%
The relationship
of motherhood is--

01:05:04.460 --> 01:05:09.260 align:middle line:84%
so the relation that is
created between two nodes that

01:05:09.260 --> 01:05:12.380 align:middle line:84%
are merged and the new node
that you create via merge,

01:05:12.380 --> 01:05:14.060 align:middle line:84%
that's the
relationship of what's

01:05:14.060 --> 01:05:15.410 align:middle line:90%
called immediate domination.

01:05:15.410 --> 01:05:18.500 align:middle line:84%
So the verb phrase immediately
dominates the V and NP.

01:05:18.500 --> 01:05:22.730 align:middle line:84%
That's just another scarier
way to say that the VP is

01:05:22.730 --> 01:05:24.620 align:middle line:90%
the mother of the V and the NP.

01:05:24.620 --> 01:05:28.880 align:middle line:84%
The VP is the thing you created
by merging V and NP together.

01:05:28.880 --> 01:05:33.350 align:middle line:84%
And dominate-- so
immediately dominate

01:05:33.350 --> 01:05:36.330 align:middle line:84%
is kind of the basic
relation in trees.

01:05:36.330 --> 01:05:38.750 align:middle line:84%
It's the relation
that's created by merge.

01:05:38.750 --> 01:05:42.260 align:middle line:84%
Dominate is the transitive
closure of immediate dominate.

01:05:42.260 --> 01:05:46.250 align:middle line:84%
So VP dominates everything
it immediately dominates,

01:05:46.250 --> 01:05:48.350 align:middle line:84%
and everything those things
immediately dominate,

01:05:48.350 --> 01:05:49.730 align:middle line:84%
and everything those
things immediately

01:05:49.730 --> 01:05:51.897 align:middle line:84%
dominate all the way down
to the bottom of the tree.

01:05:51.897 --> 01:05:54.680 align:middle line:84%
So that verb phrase
dominates the verb and the NP

01:05:54.680 --> 01:05:57.320 align:middle line:84%
and also the D and the
N-- "the" and "child."

01:05:57.320 --> 01:06:01.333 align:middle line:84%
It dominates the things that
are below it that were there

01:06:01.333 --> 01:06:02.375 align:middle line:90%
when it was put together.

01:06:02.375 --> 01:06:06.140 align:middle line:90%


01:06:06.140 --> 01:06:09.140 align:middle line:84%
And I've been using
this word "constituent,"

01:06:09.140 --> 01:06:11.120 align:middle line:84%
but now we can talk
about constituents

01:06:11.120 --> 01:06:12.170 align:middle line:90%
using this terminology.

01:06:12.170 --> 01:06:13.970 align:middle line:84%
We'll say that something
is a constituent

01:06:13.970 --> 01:06:17.030 align:middle line:84%
if all and only the
words in that thing,

01:06:17.030 --> 01:06:19.140 align:middle line:84%
alpha, are dominated
by a single node.

01:06:19.140 --> 01:06:21.950 align:middle line:84%
So there is a constituent--
"tickle the child,"

01:06:21.950 --> 01:06:23.450 align:middle line:90%
for example-- that verb phrase--

01:06:23.450 --> 01:06:25.520 align:middle line:84%
because there is
a verb phrase that

01:06:25.520 --> 01:06:28.230 align:middle line:84%
dominates just those
words, "tickle the child."

01:06:28.230 --> 01:06:31.580 align:middle line:84%
There is no constituent
"will tickle" in this tree,

01:06:31.580 --> 01:06:33.470 align:middle line:84%
because there's no node
that just dominates

01:06:33.470 --> 01:06:34.490 align:middle line:90%
the words "will tickle."

01:06:34.490 --> 01:06:40.560 align:middle line:90%


01:06:40.560 --> 01:06:42.763 align:middle line:84%
Let me-- because we are
just about out of time,

01:06:42.763 --> 01:06:45.180 align:middle line:84%
but not quite-- and this would
get us into something new--

01:06:45.180 --> 01:06:48.150 align:middle line:84%
let me get started on
getting rid of this tree,

01:06:48.150 --> 01:06:50.460 align:middle line:84%
and then we'll pick
it up here next time.

01:06:50.460 --> 01:06:54.120 align:middle line:90%
Let me show you some data.

01:06:54.120 --> 01:07:00.840 align:middle line:84%
Consider sentences
like "She likes Mary."

01:07:00.840 --> 01:07:03.050 align:middle line:90%
Let's start with that one.

01:07:03.050 --> 01:07:03.550 align:middle line:90%
No-- here.

01:07:03.550 --> 01:07:12.660 align:middle line:84%
Let's do "She thinks I like
Mary," and for that matter,

01:07:12.660 --> 01:07:17.010 align:middle line:90%
"She thinks I"--

01:07:17.010 --> 01:07:23.110 align:middle line:90%
no-- "Mary thinks I like her."

01:07:23.110 --> 01:07:25.110 align:middle line:84%
These are two grammatical
sentences in English--

01:07:25.110 --> 01:07:27.360 align:middle line:84%
"She thinks I like Mary" and
"Mary thinks I like her."

01:07:27.360 --> 01:07:30.120 align:middle line:90%


01:07:30.120 --> 01:07:33.428 align:middle line:84%
In the second sentence,
we've got "Mary"

01:07:33.428 --> 01:07:34.470 align:middle line:90%
and we've also got "her."

01:07:34.470 --> 01:07:35.935 align:middle line:90%
Can "her" refer to Mary?

01:07:35.935 --> 01:07:36.560 align:middle line:90%
AUDIENCE: Yeah.

01:07:36.560 --> 01:07:37.477 align:middle line:90%
NORVIN RICHARDS: Yeah.

01:07:37.477 --> 01:07:40.610 align:middle line:84%
It could also be somebody
else, but it could be Mary.

01:07:40.610 --> 01:07:43.880 align:middle line:84%
We'll mark that by putting a
little subscript on "Mary."

01:07:43.880 --> 01:07:45.560 align:middle line:84%
I'll call it
subscript I, and we'll

01:07:45.560 --> 01:07:47.960 align:middle line:90%
put the same subscript on her.

01:07:47.960 --> 01:07:49.820 align:middle line:84%
So "her" could refer
back to "Mary."

01:07:49.820 --> 01:07:52.280 align:middle line:84%
It could also refer
to any other person--

01:07:52.280 --> 01:07:55.270 align:middle line:90%
any other female person.

01:07:55.270 --> 01:07:56.860 align:middle line:90%
"She thinks I like Mary."

01:07:56.860 --> 01:07:59.020 align:middle line:90%
Can "she" be Mary?

01:07:59.020 --> 01:08:00.460 align:middle line:90%
No.

01:08:00.460 --> 01:08:03.490 align:middle line:90%
So here, she--

01:08:03.490 --> 01:08:05.602 align:middle line:90%
Mary can't be the same person.

01:08:05.602 --> 01:08:07.810 align:middle line:84%
It would be bad for "she"
to refer to the same person

01:08:07.810 --> 01:08:09.310 align:middle line:90%
as "Mary."

01:08:09.310 --> 01:08:10.600 align:middle line:90%
Why is that do you think?

01:08:10.600 --> 01:08:14.340 align:middle line:90%


01:08:14.340 --> 01:08:15.610 align:middle line:90%
Yeah?

01:08:15.610 --> 01:08:17.979 align:middle line:84%
AUDIENCE: Because that's
[INAUDIBLE] "I like Mary"

01:08:17.979 --> 01:08:23.140 align:middle line:84%
is a constituent, and so
when I define "Mary" inside

01:08:23.140 --> 01:08:26.479 align:middle line:84%
of the constituent-- oh,
shoot-- this is not working out.

01:08:26.479 --> 01:08:27.479 align:middle line:90%
NORVIN RICHARDS: No, no.

01:08:27.479 --> 01:08:29.133 align:middle line:90%
AUDIENCE: [INAUDIBLE].

01:08:29.133 --> 01:08:31.300 align:middle line:84%
NORVIN RICHARDS: Does anybody
have a simpler theory?

01:08:31.300 --> 01:08:31.750 align:middle line:90%
Yeah?

01:08:31.750 --> 01:08:33.958 align:middle line:84%
AUDIENCE: Is it simply
because in the first sentence,

01:08:33.958 --> 01:08:36.790 align:middle line:84%
Mary hasn't been established
as a person yet [INAUDIBLE]??

01:08:36.790 --> 01:08:38.060 align:middle line:90%
NORVIN RICHARDS: So good.

01:08:38.060 --> 01:08:40.227 align:middle line:84%
That's where I was hoping
the first person would go.

01:08:40.227 --> 01:08:43.000 align:middle line:84%
Joseph is already going
more sophisticated.

01:08:43.000 --> 01:08:46.029 align:middle line:84%
So one theory we could
have would be "Mary"

01:08:46.029 --> 01:08:49.689 align:middle line:84%
can't refer to somebody-- so
"she" can't refer to somebody

01:08:49.689 --> 01:08:51.340 align:middle line:90%
who hasn't been mentioned yet.

01:08:51.340 --> 01:08:55.750 align:middle line:84%
She can't refer to somebody
who's later in the tree.

01:08:55.750 --> 01:09:01.240 align:middle line:84%
Here, "her" is referring
to somebody on its left.

01:09:01.240 --> 01:09:09.430 align:middle line:84%
Notice-- think about a
sentence like "Everyone

01:09:09.430 --> 01:09:17.664 align:middle line:90%
who knows her likes Mary."

01:09:17.664 --> 01:09:20.680 align:middle line:90%
Can her refer to Mary there?

01:09:20.680 --> 01:09:21.310 align:middle line:90%
Yeah.

01:09:21.310 --> 01:09:24.399 align:middle line:84%
So not to immediately
dump on your hypothesis,

01:09:24.399 --> 01:09:26.810 align:middle line:84%
but I'm immediately
dumping on your hypothesis.

01:09:26.810 --> 01:09:29.770 align:middle line:84%
So pronouns actually
can refer to things

01:09:29.770 --> 01:09:32.740 align:middle line:90%
that are later in the tree.

01:09:32.740 --> 01:09:35.290 align:middle line:84%
Sorry, let me say that
again more coherently.

01:09:35.290 --> 01:09:38.050 align:middle line:84%
Pronouns can refer to things
that are later in the sentence.

01:09:38.050 --> 01:09:40.258 align:middle line:84%
You can tell I'm a syntactician,
because when I think

01:09:40.258 --> 01:09:42.790 align:middle line:90%
of sentences, I think of trees.

01:09:42.790 --> 01:09:49.810 align:middle line:84%
So the relationship that holds
between "she" and "Mary" here--

01:09:49.810 --> 01:09:53.050 align:middle line:90%
between "her" and "Mary" here--

01:09:53.050 --> 01:09:56.350 align:middle line:84%
the thing that tells you
whether a pronoun can

01:09:56.350 --> 01:09:58.040 align:middle line:90%
refer to a person--

01:09:58.040 --> 01:10:01.360 align:middle line:84%
it can't just be about whether
the person's name is later

01:10:01.360 --> 01:10:02.440 align:middle line:90%
in the sentence.

01:10:02.440 --> 01:10:04.720 align:middle line:84%
It's going to turn out
to be about the structure

01:10:04.720 --> 01:10:05.890 align:middle line:90%
of the sentence.

01:10:05.890 --> 01:10:08.140 align:middle line:84%
So we'll develop a
theory of these kinds

01:10:08.140 --> 01:10:10.630 align:middle line:84%
of facts, which
will be another test

01:10:10.630 --> 01:10:12.580 align:middle line:90%
for constituent structures.

01:10:12.580 --> 01:10:15.940 align:middle line:84%
We'll develop a theory of
that, and it will tell us

01:10:15.940 --> 01:10:19.420 align:middle line:90%
that this tree is wrong.

01:10:19.420 --> 01:10:23.620 align:middle line:84%
But I'll first finish
developing that theory,

01:10:23.620 --> 01:10:27.040 align:middle line:84%
and then we'll be able to
use it as an additional test

01:10:27.040 --> 01:10:29.710 align:middle line:90%
for structure.

01:10:29.710 --> 01:10:32.500 align:middle line:84%
That's the incentive to
come to class next time.

01:10:32.500 --> 01:10:35.290 align:middle line:84%
You'll get to see how
that's going to work.

01:10:35.290 --> 01:10:37.060 align:middle line:84%
Let's stop there
unless people have

01:10:37.060 --> 01:10:39.140 align:middle line:90%
any questions about this stuff.

01:10:39.140 --> 01:10:39.640 align:middle line:90%
Good.

01:10:39.640 --> 01:10:41.290 align:middle line:84%
Let's just pause it
right there, because if I

01:10:41.290 --> 01:10:43.040 align:middle line:84%
start trying to show
you this any further,

01:10:43.040 --> 01:10:45.240 align:middle line:90%
we'll just all get confused.

01:10:45.240 --> 01:10:49.000 align:middle line:90%